;; BUILD-GATE-1, the build smoke harness, in LLMLL. TOOL-RFC-006.
;;
;; The sixth and last port of the TOOL-LL campaign. Its subject is
;; scripts/build_smoke.sh, which is the harness that builds and runs the other
;; five ports. So this program is an LLMLL program that tests LLMLL programs.
;;
;; STATE OF THIS FILE: the spine and stages 1, 2, 2a, 3, 4, 4b, 5, 5b, 5c, 5d,
;; 5e, PROC-STDIN-1, 6, 7, 8, 9 and 10. ALL SEVENTEEN STAGE ROWS ARE PORTED.
;; 5b (FS-ENCODING-1) was the last and it was recorded as BLOCKED on
;; PROC-ENV-1, because the port could not set LC_ALL=C for a child. It sets it
;; now, through one exec of /usr/bin/env and no shell: see bs-env. **The gap
;; row does not close on that.** `wasi.proc.run` still takes no environment,
;; and a stage that reaches for a second executable to supply one is the
;; workaround the row is about. TOOL-RFC-006 section 2 tables every stage with
;; a code-line count. The RFC is the specification; this header does not
;; restate it.
;;
;; THE EXIT STATUS IS DECLARED, and it was not always. See bs-status at the
;; foot of this file: the harness used to print FAIL and exit 0. The DECISION
;; it makes is proved, in tools/build-smoke/adjudicate.llmll, and so is stage
;; 5b's three-valued verdict. This file holds the effects and the recognizers,
;; and neither can carry a proof: its results are Command values and its
;; recognizers compare strings, which falls back per STRLIT-BODY-1.
;;
;; TWELVE CONTROLS RUN AGAINST WHAT IS HERE, seven of them negative:
;;
;;   --subject <abs path>              PASS compiler present
;;   --subject stack --root <repo>     PASS compiler anchored at <resolved>
;;   full run, real fixtures           PASS stage 4, 4b and 5 lines
;;   scratch dir not clearable         PASS with NOT GRADED: wasi.fs.mkdir
;;   --subject /nonexistent/llmll      FAIL no llmll binary answered
;;   --subject stack --root /nonexist  FAIL no llmll under stack path
;;   PATH=/usr/bin:/bin                FAIL neither stack nor ghc
;;   broken fixture, STALE good Lib.hs FAIL the fixture does not build
;;   regex fixture that does not build FAIL the REGEX-LOWER-1 fixture ...
;;   regex fixture with NO call        FAIL no prefix application of regex_match
;;   capproc fixture answering WRONG   FAIL six of seven assertions, separately
;;
;; FIVE MORE RAN FOR THE PROC-STDIN-1, 6 AND 7 STAGES, four of them negative:
;;
;;   full run, real fixtures          PASS 13 lines, exit 0
;;   fixture writes not-FEEDME        FAIL the child did not read its stdin
;;                                    path, and the program printed
;;                                    FED|FED=n|EMPTY=n
;;   tamper needle that matches       FAIL the refute-crux perturbed nothing
;;   nothing                          so it proves nothing
;;   fixture :status returns 43       FAIL got code:43, so rc-is? reads the
;;                                    EXACT code and not merely non-zero
;;   any of the above, exit status    rc 1, against rc 0 for the clean run
;;
;; The last row is the one that had been missing for the whole file and not
;; only for these stages. Before it, every FAIL above exited 0.
;;
;; Each negative uses a real environment or a real input rather than a changed
;; source, so each grades these stages as they ship.
;;
;; THE WRONG-ANSWER CONTROL SHOWS THE ASSERTIONS ARE INDEPENDENT. Its fixture
;; builds, runs, prints a plausible but incorrect sha256 and a literal `exit=0`,
;; and omits the rest. Six assertions fire and name themselves; the exit=0 check
;; does NOT fire, because that string is present. Seven checks conflated into
;; one would have reported a single failure and hidden which bodies were wrong.
;;
;; TWO OF THESE ARE THE ONES THAT MATTER.
;;
;;   The stale-artifact control reproduces the exact scenario that defeated the
;;   RFC's first probe: a fixture that fails to compile, with a complete
;;   850-line generated Lib.hs left in the work directory by an earlier good
;;   run. A stage that reads the artifact without consulting the status reports
;;   PASS there. This one reports the status.
;;
;;   The no-call control fires 4b's SECOND assertion, which is the one the
;;   subject says does not decay. That fixture COMPILES, and the preamble does
;;   define regex_match, and nothing calls it. That is the "the builtin is dead
;;   again" state, and only an assertion on the CALL SITE sees it.
;;
;; ONE ASSERTION IS NOT SHOWN TO FIRE, and saying so is the point of counting.
;; 4b's third assertion is that the hyphenated `regex-match` appears ZERO times
;; in the emitted Haskell. Producing that state needs a compiler-side
;; regression, not a changed input, so no control here reaches it.
;;
;; WHY THE STATE IS A RECORD AND A CONTROL TAG, and not an int. `:mode console`
;; hands each step one Response and takes one Command back, so anything a later
;; stage needs from an earlier one has to live in the state. sequencer.llmll and
;; pathlint.llmll both carry ((Record, Ctl), Command) and this port follows
;; them. The record is Json because the alternative is a positional tuple that
;; grows a field per stage and renumbers every call site when it does.
;;
;; THE ORDERING HAZARD THAT COST THE RFC ITS FIRST PROBE, restated here because
;; it is a property of the shape rather than of that probe. A command's status
;; arrives as the Response of the NEXT step. So "run the build" and "read what
;; the build produced" are two steps, and nothing in the language forces the
;; second to consult the first. The RFC's stage 4b printed PASS against a build
;; that had exited 1, by reading a stale artifact from the previous run. In
;; shell the check is structural, because `if ! llmll build ...; then fail; fi`
;; puts the status first. Here the order is a hand-written control state.
;; EVERY STAGE MUST BRANCH ON THE STATUS BEFORE IT READS AN ARTIFACT.
;;
;; WRITE THE NEGATIVE CONTROL FIRST. A stage that has never been seen to fail is
;; not known to decide anything. This is finding 14 in tool-ll-RESTART.md, and
;; the 004 and 003 covers each found defects that a passing live run could not.
;;
;; `seq-commands` DELIVERS THE LAST COMMAND'S RESPONSE to the next step, and
;; that was measured here rather than read. LLMLL.md section 9.3 says it runs
;; its two arguments left then right; it does not say which Response arrives
;; next. Stage 4 prints its PASS line and starts 4b's build in one step, and
;; 4b then branches on the build's exit status. If the print's Response arrived
;; instead, 4b would report a build failure against a fixture that built. It
;; reports PASS, so the Response is the right-hand command's.

(module buildsmoke
  (import wasi.io   (capability stdout :deterministic false))
  (import wasi.proc (capability exec :deterministic false))
  (import wasi.fs   (capability read-write "." :deterministic false))
  ;; ENV-READ-1, shipped v0.15.0, is what makes the subject's THIRD compiler
  ;; source reachable. `:deterministic true` here is a TYPE ERROR, unlike on
  ;; every other import above, so the flag is written false explicitly rather
  ;; than omitted: the explicit form is what a reader copying this should copy.
  (import wasi.env  (capability read "HOME" :deterministic false))
  ;; TOOL-RFC-006 section 7's split. The sidecar holds the two decisions that
  ;; carry a contract; a `def` cannot call a sibling `def` in the same module,
  ;; so the split is forced by the language. `llmll check` on this file WARNS
  ;; that the core has an unproven contract until `llmll verify` has run on the
  ;; sidecar, so the trust comes from running verify in the job and not from a
  ;; green check here.
  (import adjudicate)
  (open adjudicate)

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------ state --
  ;; Json carries the accumulated verdicts so a field added by stage 9 does not
  ;; renumber stage 1's call sites.
  (type Bs (| Bs Json))
  (def-shell bs-of [b: Bs] -> Json (match b ((Bs j) j)))
  (def-shell bs-mk [j: Json] -> Bs (Bs j))

  ;; `sets` and `get-s` do not exist as builtins and are written locally at
  ;; every port in this campaign. json-set answers a Result, so the unwrap
  ;; keeps the old value rather than dropping the write silently.
  (def-shell sets [v: Json k: string s: string] -> Json
    (unwrap-or (json-set v k (json-of-string s)) v))

  (def-shell with-s [b: Bs k: string s: string] -> Bs (bs-mk (sets (bs-of b) k s)))
  (def-shell get-s  [b: Bs k: string] -> string
    (unwrap-or (json-get-string (bs-of b) k) ""))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------- argv --
  ;; THE ARGUMENT CONTRACT, and it exists because the subject reads an
  ;; ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE that no builtin can read.
  ;;
  ;;   --subject  the llmll compiler. Default "llmll", found on PATH.
  ;;   --root     the repository root. Default "."
  ;;   --work     a scratch directory for build outputs. Default "."
  ;;
  ;; The names follow refutecrux.llmll, which already takes exactly these three
  ;; and whose CI step passes an absolute --subject. Inventing a fourth spelling
  ;; for the same three ideas would make the campaign's ports differ for no
  ;; reason.
  ;;
  ;; ALL THREE SOURCES ARE NOW REACHED. `scripts/build_smoke.sh:116-126` tries
  ;; three for the compiler in order: $LLMLL_BIN, then `command -v llmll`, then
  ;; $HOME/.local/bin/llmll. `--subject` reproduces the first and a bare
  ;; "llmll" reproduces the second. The third needed $HOME.
  ;;
  ;; **It was unreachable until v0.15.0 and this comment used to say so.** The
  ;; campaign census carried "no env access" as COSMETIC because "argv carries
  ;; it". That held for port 005 and it FAILED here, because argv carries what
  ;; a caller passes and $HOME is not passed. Reading it through
  ;; `sh -c 'echo $HOME'` would break D1's rule that this port hands no string
  ;; to a shell, so the branch was dropped rather than faked. `ENV-READ-1`
  ;; shipped `wasi.env.get` and the branch is now ported; the phases are Home2
  ;; and Comp2b below.
  ;;
  ;; **The lesson is the more useful half: a disposition tested against one
  ;; port measures that port, not the language.**
  (def-shell argv-of [x: Response] -> list[string]
    (match x
      ((RList as) as)
      ((RNone)    (list-empty))
      ((RText _)  (list-empty))
      ((RCode _)  (list-empty))
      ((RErr _)   (list-empty))))

  (def-shell flag-value [as: list[string] f: string] -> string
    (first (list-fold as (pair "" false)
             (fn [acc: (string, bool) a: string]
               (if (second acc)
                   (pair a false)
                   (if (= a f) (pair (first acc) true) acc))))))

  (def-shell flag-or [as: list[string] f: string d: string] -> string
    (let [(v (flag-value as f))] (if (string-empty? v) d v)))

  ;; One tag per stage, plus the two terminal phases. `:done?` is read on the
  ;; state a step RETURNS and that step's Command is DISCARDED, so a phase must
  ;; not both print and finish. Ending prints; Done finishes.
  (type Ctl
    (| Boot)
    (| Tool1)      ;; stage 1, toolchain present
    (| Tool1Ghc)   ;; stage 1, the ghc fallback the subject also accepts
    (| Comp2)      ;; stage 2, an llmll binary answers
    ;; Stage 2's THIRD source, ported at v0.15.0. Two phases and not one,
    ;; because reading $HOME and running what it points at are separate steps:
    ;; the env read answers on the response channel like any other Command.
    (| Home2)      ;; asked for $HOME; RText or RErr arrives
    (| Comp2b)     ;; ran <home>/.local/bin/llmll version; status arrives
    ;; Stage 2a takes FIVE phases and not two, and the reason is the shape
    ;; rather than the task. `wasi.proc.run` answers the child's EXIT STATUS as
    ;; RCode; the child's stdout went to the path the call named. So reading
    ;; what `stack path` printed is a SEPARATE step from learning whether it
    ;; succeeded, and each source needs both.
    (| Anchor2a)   ;; ran `stack path` in the working directory; status arrives
    (| Anchor2aR)  ;; read that root from its file
    (| Anchor2aC)  ;; ran `stack path` in <root>/compiler; status arrives
    (| Anchor2aCR) ;; read that root from its file
    ;; TWO execute-checks and not one, because the subject's fallback condition
    ;; is a DISJUNCTION: `[ -z "$_SIR" ] || [ ! -x "$_SIR/bin/llmll" ]`. A root
    ;; that resolves but holds no compiler must still fall through to
    ;; <root>/compiler. A single shared check cannot, because it does not know
    ;; which source it is checking, and collapsing them reported FAIL against a
    ;; tree whose compiler was built and present.
    (| Anchor2aX1) ;; first source's binary: on failure, try <root>/compiler
    (| Anchor2aX2) ;; second source's binary: on failure, the stage fails
    (| Build3)     ;; stage 3, `llmll build` on the fixture; status arrives
    (| Lib4)       ;; stage 4, read the emitted src/Lib.hs
    (| Log4)       ;; stage 4, read the build log for a GHC invocation
    (| Build4b)    ;; stage 4b, build the REGEX-LOWER-1 fixture; status arrives
    (| Lib4b)      ;; stage 4b, read its emitted Lib.hs and assert on the text
    ;; Stage 5 is the first stage that RUNS what it built, so it needs the
    ;; whole exe_path mechanism and a stdin file for the run.
    (| Build5)     ;; built capproc_exec; status arrives
    (| Sir5)       ;; ran `stack path` in the fixture's own outdir
    (| Sir5R)      ;; read that root and form the binary path
    (| Bin5)       ;; listed that root's bin/ to decide the binary EXISTS
    (| Feed5)      ;; wrote the run's stdin file
    (| Del5in)     ;; cleared the scratch directory: in.txt
    (| Del5out)    ;; cleared the scratch directory: out.txt
    (| Del5err)    ;; cleared the scratch directory: err.txt
    (| Rmd5)       ;; removed the scratch directory itself
    (| Pre5)       ;; listed the scratch directory BEFORE the run
    (| Run5)       ;; ran the binary; status arrives
    (| Out5)       ;; read the merged output
    (| Post5)      ;; listed the scratch directory AFTER the run
    ;; stage 5b, FS-ENCODING-1 and FS-COPY-1. Twenty phases, and fifteen of
    ;; them are the stage's own PRECONDITION: the scratch directory is cleared
    ;; file by file, remade, and then given the two inputs the fixture cannot
    ;; author for itself. The subject gets all of that from `rm -rf`, `mkdir
    ;; -p`, one `printf` and one `awk`.
    (| Fe)         ;; built fs_encoding.llmll; status arrives
    (| FeS)        ;; ran `stack path` in its out dir
    (| FeR)        ;; read that install root; start clearing the scratch dir
    (| FeD1)       ;; deleted text.txt
    (| FeD2)       ;; deleted bin.dat
    (| FeD3)       ;; deleted bin.copy
    (| FeD4)       ;; deleted big.txt
    (| FeD5)       ;; deleted big.copy
    (| FeRm)       ;; removed the scratch directory itself
    (| FeMk)       ;; made it again
    (| FeBin)      ;; wrote the BINARY input through printf; status arrives
    (| FeBig)      ;; wrote the multi-buffer input and the run's stdin file
    (| FePlat)     ;; ran `uname -s`; status arrives
    (| FeUn)       ;; read the platform name and fixed the verdict it allows
    (| FeRun)      ;; ran the fixture under LC_ALL=C
    (| FeOut)      ;; read its merged output
    (| FeH1)       ;; hashed bin.dat
    (| FeH2)       ;; hashed bin.copy
    (| FeH3)       ;; hashed big.txt
    (| FeH4)       ;; hashed big.copy; the verdict
    ;; stage 5c, CAPTURE-ENCODING-1
    (| Cenc)       ;; built capture_encoding.llmll; status arrives
    (| CencS)      ;; ran `stack path`
    (| CencR)      ;; read that install root; wrote the stdin file
    (| CencRun)    ;; ran the fixture; status arrives
    (| CencOd)     ;; ran `od` over its output; status arrives
    (| CencP)      ;; read the hex dump
    ;; stage 5d, JSON-SCALAR-1
    (| Js)         ;; built json_scalar.llmll; status arrives
    (| JsS)        ;; ran `stack path`
    (| JsR)        ;; read that install root; wrote the stdin file
    (| JsRun)      ;; ran the fixture; status arrives
    (| JsP)        ;; read its output
    ;; stage 5e, PROC-MERGE-1
    (| Pm)         ;; built proc_merge.llmll; status arrives
    (| PmS)        ;; ran `stack path`
    (| PmR)        ;; read that install root; wrote the stdin file
    (| PmRun)      ;; ran the fixture; status arrives
    (| PmP)        ;; read that install root; wrote the stdin file
    (| PmDone)     ;; ran the fixture; status arrives
    (| PmOut)      ;; read its output
    (| PmFin)      ;; both stream-shape lines asserted
    ;; PROC-STDIN-1, the subject's unnumbered stage
    (| Ps)         ;; built proc_stdin.llmll; status arrives
    (| PsS)        ;; ran `stack path` in its out dir
    (| PsR)        ;; read that install root; made the run dir and the feed
    (| PsRun)      ;; ran the fixture against 147456 bytes of stdin
    (| PsOut)      ;; read its output
    (| PsFin)      ;; both halves asserted
    ;; stage 6, REPLAY-FRAME. Twelve phases entered TWICE, then four
    ;; witnesses. Rp is the entry and RpTRun is the re-entry point.
    (| Rp)         ;; made the replay dir; resolve fixture 0 and copy it
    (| RpCp)       ;; the copy answered; build it
    (| RpB)        ;; built it; status arrives
    (| RpS)        ;; ran `stack path` in its generated dir
    (| RpR)        ;; read that install root; wrote the two-line feed
    (| RpF)        ;; the feed is written; RECORD the run
    (| RpRun)      ;; the recorded run finished; read its event log
    (| RpLog)      ;; the event log arrived; REPLAY it
    (| RpRep)      ;; replay finished; read what it printed
    (| RpOut)      ;; asserted 2/2; TAMPER the log
    (| RpTam)      ;; the tampered log is written; replay THAT
    (| RpTRun)     ;; the tampered replay must FAIL; then re-enter or advance
    (| RpWi)       ;; replayed replay_settle for its warning
    (| RpWiP)      ;; W-REPLAY-INIT must be present; replay replay-demo
    (| RpWd)       ;; that replay finished; read its output
    (| RpFin)      ;; W-REPLAY-INIT must be ABSENT there
    ;; stage 7, PROC-BOUNDARY-1. Seven child runs and their exit codes.
    (| Pb)         ;; built proc_boundary.llmll; status arrives
    (| PbS)        ;; ran `stack path` in its out dir
    (| PbR)        ;; read that install root; wrote the two-line feed
    (| PbF2)       ;; wrote the one-line feed
    (| PbF1)       ;; ran (a), the :done? path
    (| PbA)        ;; (a) must exit 42; run (b) with three arguments
    (| PbAr)       ;; (b) finished; read what it printed
    (| PbArP)      ;; argc=3 asserted; run (c) with none
    (| PbA0)       ;; (c) finished; read what it printed
    (| PbA0P)      ;; argc=0 asserted; run (d), the STARVED stdin
    (| PbSt)       ;; (d) must exit 70; clear the event log
    (| PbDel)      ;; the log is cleared; run (e), immediate EOF
    (| PbEof)      ;; (e) must exit 70; read the event log
    (| PbHdr)      ;; the header must have survived; run replay-demo
    (| PbRd)       ;; a no-:done? program must exit 0; run it on empty stdin
    (| PbRd0)      ;; and 0 again on immediate EOF
    ;; stage 8, the DRIVER-LL 4a+4b+4c cover
    (| Drv8)       ;; built sequencer.llmll; status arrives
    (| Drv8S)      ;; ran `stack path` in the sequencer's out dir
    (| Drv8R)      ;; read that install root
    (| Drv8P)      ;; ran driver_ll_cover.py; status arrives
    ;; stage 9, the DRIVER-LL 4e wave cover
    (| Wav9)       ;; built wave.llmll; status arrives
    (| Wav9S)      ;; ran `stack path` in the wave's out dir
    (| Wav9R)      ;; read that install root
    (| Wav9P)      ;; ran wave_cover.py; status arrives
    ;; stage 10, DRIFT-CI-1 decided by an LLMLL program
    (| Vg10)       ;; built versiongate.llmll; status arrives
    (| Vg10S)      ;; ran `stack path` in the version gate's out dir
    (| Vg10R)      ;; read that install root
    (| Vg10C)      ;; ran version_gate_cover.py; status arrives
    (| Vg10F)      ;; wrote the live run's stdin file
    (| Vg10L)      ;; ran the gate against the LIVE tree; status arrives
    (| Ending)
    (| Done))

  (def-shell go [b: Bs p: Ctl c: Command] -> ((Bs, Ctl), Command)
    (pair (pair b p) c))

  ;; Stage 2 and stage 2a both end by handing control to stage 3, from three
  ;; different phases. One entry point rather than three copies, because a
  ;; fourth path added later would otherwise be the one that forgets.
  (def-shell start-build [b: Bs] -> ((Bs, Ctl), Command)
    (go b Build3
        (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                       ["build" (fixture-of (get-s b "root"))
                        "-o" (get-s b "work")]
                       (get-s b "root")
                       (log-of (get-s b "work")) (log-of (get-s b "work"))
                       900 "/dev/null")))

  ;; THE ONE ENVIRONMENT-BEARING SPAWN, and every site that needs one goes
  ;; through here. `wasi.proc.run` takes no environment (`PROC-ENV-1`, open),
  ;; and stage 5b needs `LC_ALL=C` for exactly one child.
  ;;
  ;; WHY THIS IS NOT `sh -c`, which is the whole point of writing it. D1 bounds
  ;; this port to handing NO string to a shell, and `/usr/bin/env` keeps that
  ;; bound: it is a POSIX executable, `wasi.proc.run` splits the executable from
  ;; its arguments syntactically, and each assignment is ONE argv member. No
  ;; shell reads the string, so no metacharacter is interpreted, and a value
  ;; carrying a space or a `;` reaches the child unchanged. `env` then execs in
  ;; place, so the child keeps the stdin the call named: MEASURED, because a
  ;; wrapper that forked would have cost stage 5b the twelve lines it feeds.
  ;;
  ;; The argv is `assigns ++ [exe] ++ args` and it is built by a FOLD, which is
  ;; not decoration. MEASURED against llmll 0.16.0: `list-append` is
  ;; `list[a] -> a -> list[a]`, appending ONE ELEMENT, and no builtin
  ;; concatenates two lists. So the element append is the only join there is.
  (def-shell bs-env [assigns: list[string] exe: string args: list[string]
                     cwd: string out: string err: string tmo: int stdin: string] -> Command
    (wasi.proc.run "/usr/bin/env"
                   (list-fold args (list-append assigns exe)
                              (fn [acc: list[string] a: string] (list-append acc a)))
                   cwd out err tmo stdin))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------- responses --
  ;; Every shape is rendered distinctly. Collapsing two shapes to "" is how
  ;; JSON-SCALAR-1 went unnoticed for a corpus run: the port reported 30 passed
  ;; and 50 failed, and 50 was the count of cases whose flags had been dropped.
  (def-shell bs-text [r: Response] -> string
    (match r
      ((RText t)  t)
      ((RErr e)   e)
      ((RNone)    "")
      ((RCode n)  (string-concat "code:" (int-to-string n)))
      ((RList xs) "")))

  ;; PROBED 2026-08-12 by scripts/build-smoke/d3_probe.llmll, against llmll
  ;; 0.14.99, and recorded in TOOL-RFC-006 D3. A failed exec answers RErr and
  ;; the step machine survives it. The two causes carry DIFFERENT text:
  ;;
  ;;   missing         "...: createProcess: execvp: does not exist (No such file
  ;;                    or directory)"
  ;;   not executable  "...: createProcess: execvp: permission denied
  ;;                    (Permission denied)"
  ;;
  ;; The subject's `command -v` and its `-perm -111` test each answer one bit
  ;; and cannot say which happened. Reproducing the reference does not require
  ;; discarding that, because the subject's exe_path prints two DIFFERENT notes
  ;; for its two causes and this is how the port earns them.
  (def-shell absent? [r: Response] -> bool
    (string-contains (bs-text r) "does not exist"))

  (def-shell denied? [r: Response] -> bool
    (string-contains (bs-text r) "permission denied"))

  ;; A child that exited 0. RCode carries the status; every other shape from a
  ;; `wasi.proc.run` is a failure to launch, which `absent?` and `denied?`
  ;; classify. Written as its own predicate because "the command succeeded" and
  ;; "the artifact says what I expect" are the two halves the RFC's first probe
  ;; conflated, and conflating them is how it printed PASS against exit 1.
  (def-shell ok? [r: Response] -> bool
    (match r
      ((RCode n)  (= n 0))
      ((RText _)  false)
      ((RErr _)   false)
      ((RNone)    false)
      ((RList _)  false)))

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- report --
  ;; The subject's two verdict spellings, at scripts/build_smoke.sh:57 for FAIL
  ;; and at each stage's own echo for PASS. A report text must not end with a
  ;; line end; the harness adds one.
  (def-shell pass-line [what: string] -> string
    (string-concat "BUILD-GATE-1 PASS: " what))

  (def-shell fail-line [what: string] -> string
    (string-concat "BUILD-GATE-1 FAIL: " what))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------- stage 1, toolchain --
  ;; scripts/build_smoke.sh:107. The subject fails closed when NEITHER stack nor
  ;; ghc is on PATH, and its comment says why a skip would be wrong: this gate
  ;; compiles generated Haskell, and runGhcCheck returns True when no toolchain
  ;; is present (compiler/app/Main.hs:940), so a skip would pass while observing
  ;; nothing. That is finding 6's class and the subject already refuses it.
  ;;
  ;; The port asks each binary for its version rather than asking PATH for its
  ;; presence, because no builtin answers "is this name on PATH". D3 established
  ;; that the failure is observable and non-fatal, which is what makes the
  ;; substitution sound.
  (def-shell tool-fail [] -> string
    (fail-line "neither 'stack' nor 'ghc' answers --version. This gate compiles generated Haskell; without a toolchain it would pass while observing nothing. Install Stack from https://haskellstack.org, or set PATH, and re-run."))

  ;; ---------------------------------------- stage 2, the compiler binary --
  ;; scripts/build_smoke.sh:118. A missing compiler is a FAILURE here and not a
  ;; skip, for the same reason stage 1 gives.
  (def-shell comp-fail [subj: string] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["BUILD-GATE-1 FAIL: no llmll binary answered '" subj
       " version'. Pass --subject with a path to the compiler, or put llmll on PATH. A missing binary is a failure here, not a skip."]))

  ;; ------------------------------- stage 2a, anchor to an ABSOLUTE path --
  ;; scripts/build_smoke.sh:126, and the subject's comment there is the reason
  ;; rather than a preference. Later stages run the compiler from a DIFFERENT
  ;; directory: the replay stage works inside the scratch tree and the
  ;; DRIVER-LL stage works inside tools/llmll-driver. A relative invocation
  ;; re-resolves against that new directory and dies there. The subject
  ;; measured it in CI run 30938236092, where stack took the temp output
  ;; directory for its project root because that is where it was standing.
  ;;
  ;; The port hits this through `--subject stack` rather than through
  ;; LLMLL_BIN="stack exec llmll --", because argv carries one word per member
  ;; and the trailing `--` only ever separated stack's flags from the
  ;; compiler's. Resolving once here, while the working directory is still the
  ;; caller's, makes every later invocation independent of the directory.
  (def-shell stack-shaped? [subj: string] -> bool
    (or (= subj "stack") (string-contains subj "/stack")))

  (def-shell bin-under [root: string] -> string
    (string-concat root "/bin/llmll"))

  ;; ---------------------------------------- stage 2, the THIRD source ----
  ;; scripts/build_smoke.sh:120, `[ -x "$HOME/.local/bin/llmll" ]`.
  (def-shell home-bin [h: string] -> string
    (string-concat h "/.local/bin/llmll"))

  ;; THE FIDELITY DECISION, and it is deliberate rather than convenient.
  ;;
  ;; The subject is bash, so an UNSET $HOME expands to the EMPTY STRING and the
  ;; test becomes `[ -x "/.local/bin/llmll" ]`. `wasi.env.get` answers RErr for
  ;; unset, so this port CAN tell unset from set and the reference cannot.
  ;;
  ;; The port reproduces the subject's BEHAVIOUR and keeps the language's
  ;; DISTINCTION for the diagnostic only. `home-of` maps RErr to "", so the
  ;; path formed is the same path the reference tests. `home-unset?` keeps the
  ;; fact, and only the failure message spends it.
  ;;
  ;; The alternative was to FAIL on RErr. That diverges from the reference on a
  ;; real input, and the differential cover grades per-stage agreement, so a
  ;; port that answered differently here would be measuring its own opinion.
  ;; Collapsing RErr to "" and FORGETTING it was the other alternative, and
  ;; that is the JSON-SCALAR-1 shape this file's response section rejects.
  (def-shell home-of [r: Response] -> string
    (match r
      ((RText h) h)
      ((RErr _)  "")
      ((RNone)   "")
      ((RCode _) "")
      ((RList _) "")))

  (def-shell home-unset? [r: Response] -> bool
    (match r
      ((RText _) false)
      ((RErr _)  true)
      ((RNone)   true)
      ((RCode _) true)
      ((RList _) true)))

  ;; The third source's own failure line. It is NOT comp-fail: that one names
  ;; the subject the caller passed, and by this point the subject is a path
  ;; this port derived. Reusing it would report a path the caller never wrote.
  (def-shell comp3-fail [path: string unset: bool] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["BUILD-GATE-1 FAIL: no llmll binary answered. The last source tried was '"
       path "', which did not answer 'version'."
       (if unset
           " HOME IS UNSET, and that path is what the reference's own \"$HOME/.local/bin/llmll\" expands to with HOME empty; the reference cannot say this and this port can."
           "")
       " Pass --subject with a path to the compiler, or put llmll on PATH. A missing binary is a failure here, not a skip."]))

  (def-shell anchor-fail [] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["BUILD-GATE-1 FAIL: --subject names stack but no llmll binary exists under `stack path --local-install-root`, from either the working directory or <root>/compiler. Build it first: (cd compiler && stack build)."]))

  ;; `stack path` writes the root and a line end. A path with a line end inside
  ;; it is not a path, and the failure it causes names the wrong thing.
  ;; string-split takes the SEPARATOR FIRST; the reversed call type-checks and
  ;; returns a malformed result, which cost port 004 five call sites.
  (def-shell chomp [s: string] -> string
    (unwrap-or (list-head (string-split "\n" s)) s))

  ;; --------------------------------------------- stages 3 and 4, the build --
  ;; scripts/build_smoke.sh:175 and :186. Stage 3 builds the fixture. Stage 4
  ;; corroborates INDEPENDENTLY that something was compiled, and its comment
  ;; names the class it guards: a fail-open runGhcCheck reporting success on an
  ;; emitted file that never reached GHC.
  (def-shell fixture-of [root: string] -> string
    (string-concat root "/scripts/build-smoke/smoke.llmll"))

  (def-shell lib-of [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/src/Lib.hs"))
  (def-shell log-of [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.build.log"))

  ;; The subject's list at scripts/build_smoke.sh:202, verbatim and in its
  ;; order. Each name must carry a TOP-LEVEL definition in the emitted
  ;; preamble, which the subject tests as `^name ` and this tests as a line
  ;; start followed by the name and a space. A literal list is also what
  ;; SPLIT-EMPTY-1 forces: `string-split ""` does not terminate, so a scan is
  ;; a fold over a written-out list.
  ;;
  ;; IT WAS NOT VERBATIM, AND THE PORT WAS THE WEAKER SIDE. The difference was
  ;; measured in both directions: thirty names in the subject against
  ;; twenty-eight here, `wasi_env_get` and `wasi_fs_copy` missing from this
  ;; list, and nothing here that the subject omits. ENV-READ-1 added both to
  ;; the subject after this list was copied. `wasi_env_get` only shipped at
  ;; v0.15.0, so the port's list predates it; `wasi_fs_copy` is NOT new, and
  ;; smoke.llmll has called wasi.fs.copy since FS-COPY-1. A hand-maintained
  ;; list drifts in silence, which is the subject's own warning at :192 and is
  ;; now this file's too.
  ;;
  ;; BUILTIN-BODY-1 added `sha1_hash`, the first non-wasi_* name on either side,
  ;; and it is added HERE IN THE SAME CHANGE as the subject rather than being
  ;; discovered later by a count. That is the whole lesson of the paragraph
  ;; above: the two names it names went missing because the subject moved and
  ;; this list did not.
  ;; BUILTIN-BODY-1 residue (1), 2026-09-06: the six preamble names the
  ;; hand-written equations reach, added when smoke.llmll gained a call to each,
  ;; in the same change as the reference list, per the paragraph above.
  (def-shell preamble-names [] -> list[string]
    ["wasi_io_stdout" "wasi_io_stderr" "wasi_http_response"
     "wasi_fs_read" "wasi_fs_write" "wasi_fs_delete" "wasi_http_post"
     "wasi_fs_list" "wasi_fs_mkdir" "wasi_fs_sha256"
     "wasi_clock_monotonic" "wasi_proc_run" "wasi_proc_args"
     "wasi_env_get" "wasi_fs_copy"
     "sha1_hash"
     "bytes_length" "bytes_get" "bytes_set" "list_nth" "string_slice" "string_char_at"
     "seq_commands"
     "json_parse" "json_serialize" "json_get" "json_get_string" "json_get_int"
     "json_get_bool" "json_get_number" "json_array" "json_object" "json_set"
     "json_of_string" "json_of_int" "json_of_bool" "json_of_list"])

  (def-shell defines? [lib: string n: string] -> bool
    (string-contains lib (string-concat "\n" (string-concat n " "))))

  (def-shell missing-in [lib: string] -> list[string]
    (list-filter (preamble-names) (fn [n: string] (not (defines? lib n)))))

  ;; The subject accepts either spelling, because the two backends log
  ;; differently and a gate that knew only one would call a real GHC run a
  ;; fail-open path.
  (def-shell ghc-ran? [lg: string] -> bool
    (or (string-contains lg "stack build OK") (string-contains lg "ghc OK")))

  ;; -------------------------------------------- stage 4b, REGEX-LOWER-1 --
  ;; scripts/build_smoke.sh:222. THIS STAGE IS THE ONLY GATE THAT CAN SEE ITS
  ;; DEFECT CLASS. `regex-match` passed `llmll check` at exit 0 with no warning
  ;; and `llmll verify` as SAFE, then failed at GHC: `isOperator` in Parser.hs
  ;; named it, and `emitOp`'s fallback put the operator name BETWEEN its
  ;; arguments. A check-time fixture is green on both sides of that fix, so
  ;; only a build sees it.
  ;;
  ;; Two assertions, and the second does not decay. Building is necessary, but
  ;; a later refactor could make the module build while emitting something other
  ;; than the preamble binding, so the call site is asserted directly.
  (def-shell rx-fixture-of [root: string] -> string
    (string-concat root "/scripts/build-smoke/regex_lower.llmll"))

  (def-shell rx-out-of [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/regex-lower"))
  (def-shell rx-log-of [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.regex-lower.log"))
  (def-shell rx-lib-of [work: string] -> string
    (string-concat (rx-out-of work) "/src/Lib.hs"))

  ;; The PREFIX APPLICATION and not merely the preamble definition. If nothing
  ;; calls it the builtin is dead again, which is the state this gate exists to
  ;; detect.
  (def-shell rx-applied? [lib: string] -> bool
    (string-contains lib "regex_match ("))

  ;; ZERO occurrences of the hyphenated spelling, and the subject's comment
  ;; records why the count is zero rather than a pattern. An earlier assertion
  ;; matched `[a-z_]+ regex-match `, modelled on the `(p regex-match s)` form.
  ;; It was WRONG BY CONSTRUCTION: this fixture's first def passes a string
  ;; LITERAL, so the emission is `("^[a-f0-9]{64}$" regex-match s)` and the
  ;; character before the operator is a quote rather than an identifier. The
  ;; pattern missed the very call it was written for. Measured on both sides
  ;; instead: a correct build contains none at all.
  (def-shell rx-hyphen? [lib: string] -> bool
    (string-contains lib "regex-match"))

  ;; ----------------------------------------------- stage 5, CAP-PROC --
  ;; scripts/build_smoke.sh:281. Everything above proves the preamble COMPILES,
  ;; and that is not the property WASI-RT was about. The stopgap `wasi.fs.read`
  ;; performed a read and discarded the result: it compiled, it linked, it
  ;; raised nothing on an unreadable path, and it passed every check above.
  ;; `readFile` is lazy, a spawned process can go unwaited, and a "hash" can be
  ;; a rolling polynomial returning twenty plausible bytes. `sha1_hash` in the
  ;; same preamble is exactly that and shipped for four releases. GHC sees none
  ;; of it. So this stage RUNS a second fixture and compares against answers
  ;; computed outside the program.
  (def-shell x-fixture-of [root: string] -> string
    (string-concat root "/scripts/build-smoke/capproc_exec.llmll"))

  (def-shell x-out-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/exec"))
  (def-shell x-log-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.exec-build.log"))
  (def-shell x-sir-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.exec-sir.txt"))
  (def-shell x-feed-of [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.exec-stdin.txt"))
  (def-shell x-run-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.exec-run.txt"))

  ;; Literal /tmp and not a temporary directory the port chooses. The FIXTURE
  ;; hardcodes this path, because LLMLL cannot read an environment variable,
  ;; and its `(capability read-write "/tmp")` clause names the same root. The
  ;; subject's own comment says so. Deriving it here would check a directory
  ;; the program never touches.
  (def-shell x-scratch [] -> string "/tmp/llmll-capproc-exec")

  ;; The console harness consumes ONE STDIN LINE PER STEP, so this is the step
  ;; budget and not data. The subject feeds twelve. This is the first stage
  ;; that needs a child's stdin at all, and `PROC-STDIN-1` is what makes it a
  ;; path rather than a shell string.
  (def-shell x-feed-text [] -> string "x\nx\nx\nx\nx\nx\nx\nx\nx\nx\nx\nx\n")

  ;; SHA-256("abc"), FIPS 180-4. A stub that derives bytes from its input
  ;; passes every other assertion in the subject and fails this one.
  (def-shell x-expect-sha [] -> string
    "sha256=ba7816bf8f01cfea414140de5dae2223b00361a396177a9cb410ff61f20015ad")

  ;; One check per assertion, each answering "did this fail". The subject
  ;; collects failures into an array and prints all of them, so the port
  ;; collects the same way: reporting only the first would make a two-defect
  ;; run look like a one-defect run.
  (def-shell x-miss [out: string] -> list[string]
    (list-filter
      [(if (string-contains out (x-expect-sha)) ""
           "wasi.fs.sha256 did not produce the NIST vector for \"abc\"")
       (if (string-contains out "exit=0") ""
           "wasi.proc.run did not report exit 0")
       (if (string-contains out "echo=capproc-echo-ok") ""
           "wasi.proc.run's child output did not round-trip through the redirect file")
       ;; POSIX ERE, and the pattern comes FIRST. TDFA has no \d.
       (if (regex-match "clock=[0-9]+" out) ""
           "wasi.clock.monotonic did not return a positive integer")
       (if (string-contains out "json=jr=42") ""
           "the JSON round trip did not yield jr=42; json-set, json-serialize, json-parse, or a typed projection is wrong")
       (if (string-contains out "dup=rejected") ""
           "json-parse accepted an object with duplicate member names (RFC 7493 2.3)")
       (if (string-contains out "battery=ARRRRRRAAR") ""
           "the adversarial parse battery did not match ARRRRRRAAR; json-parse accepts or rejects the wrong inputs")]
      (fn [m: string] (not (string-empty? m)))))

  (def-shell x-report [ms: list[string]] -> string
    (string-concat-many (list-map ms (fn [m: string] (string-concat "; " m)))))

  ;; A directory that a listing can read. `wasi.fs.list` on an absent path
  ;; answers RErr, which is the same substitution D3 established for exec:
  ;; no builtin answers "is there a file here" (FS-EXISTS-1), so the operation
  ;; that needs it answers instead.
  (def-shell listed? [r: Response] -> bool
    (match r
      ((RList _)  true)
      ((RText _)  true)
      ((RNone)    true)
      ((RCode _)  false)
      ((RErr _)   false)))

  ;; Does a directory hold an entry of this name? The second half of the
  ;; subject's `exe_path`, which asks `[ ! -x "$p" ]` and this cannot.
  ;;
  ;; P-C1, MEASURED 2026-08-16 against llmll 0.16.0 before this was written,
  ;; because measurement 3 of design/tool-ll-RESTART.md is owed to this fix.
  ;; `wasi.fs.list` on a directory holding exactly one file answered
  ;; `RList n=1` with `list-contains` true for that file's name, agreeing with
  ;; the shell's own listing on both the count and the membership. So the
  ;; entries are BASENAMES, not paths, and containment on the basename decides
  ;; existence. The runtime is `listDirectory` followed by `sort`, which is
  ;; where the basenames come from.
  ;;
  ;; EVERY NON-RList ARM IS false, and the RErr arm is the one that carries
  ;; weight: `wasi.fs.list` on an absent path answers RErr, so a missing bin/
  ;; and a present bin/ with no such entry both answer false here. Those are
  ;; the same verdict for the subject too, whose two internal notes differ
  ;; while the `fail` they reach is one message.
  ;;
  ;; -x IS NOT REPRODUCED. A listing decides existence and says nothing about
  ;; the permission bit, so a present-but-unexecutable binary passes here and
  ;; fails the subject. That is a real difference and it is narrower than the
  ;; one it replaces: without this check the port ran a binary that was not
  ;; there at all. FS-STAT-1 is the row that would close it.
  (def-shell has-entry? [r: Response n: string] -> bool
    (match r
      ((RList xs) (list-contains xs n))
      ((RText _)  false)
      ((RNone)    false)
      ((RCode _)  false)
      ((RErr _)   false)))

  ;; --------------------------------------- stage 5b, FS-ENCODING-1 --
  ;; scripts/build_smoke.sh:368-499. Two properties that only a RUNNING program
  ;; settles, and that were both asserted from source before they were measured.
  ;; The subject's own comment carries the history; this one carries what the
  ;; port does differently and why.
  ;;
  ;; THE VERDICT IS THREE-VALUED and the third value is the reason this stage
  ;; was worth porting at all. See fsenc-verdict in adjudicate.llmll: the five
  ;; assertions are graded everywhere, and the LC_ALL=C CLAIM is printed only
  ;; where GHC honours LC_ALL. On macOS it does not (GHC 9.6.6 aarch64-osx
  ;; resolves UTF-8 under LC_ALL=C while `locale charmap` reports US-ASCII), so
  ;; the encoding half is a structural no-op there. It reported PASS on the
  ;; developer's machine for the whole of v0.14.84 while the property it named
  ;; was false, and its first real execution failed on the first line it
  ;; printed.
  ;;
  ;; THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE PLATFORM AND NOT A PROBE, and the subject refuses
  ;; the probe on purpose: after the fix the program behaves IDENTICALLY under
  ;; both locales, so nothing it prints distinguishes them. A port that read the
  ;; fixture's own output to decide would be asking the thing under test.
  (def-shell fe-fixture-of [root: string] -> string
    (string-concat root "/scripts/build-smoke/fs_encoding.llmll"))

  (def-shell fe-out-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/fsenc"))
  (def-shell fe-log-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.fsenc-build.log"))
  (def-shell fe-sir-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.fsenc-sir.txt"))
  (def-shell fe-feed-of [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.fsenc-stdin.txt"))
  (def-shell fe-run-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.fsenc-run.txt"))
  (def-shell fe-pf-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.fsenc-printf.log"))
  (def-shell fe-un-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.fsenc-uname.txt"))

  ;; Literal /tmp, on the same ground as x-scratch: the FIXTURE hardcodes this
  ;; path and its `(capability read-write "/tmp")` clause names the same root.
  (def-shell fe-scratch [] -> string "/tmp/llmll-fsenc")
  (def-shell fe-at [n: string] -> string (string-concat-many [(fe-scratch) "/" n]))

  ;; THE BINARY INPUT IS SPAWNED AND NOT WRITTEN, and it is the one place in
  ;; this file where an exec substitutes for a builtin the language does not
  ;; have. bin.dat carries a lone 0xFF; a .llmll string literal cannot, which is
  ;; why the fixture cannot author its own adversarial input and says so in its
  ;; header. `wasi.fs.write` takes a string and encodes it as UTF-8, so there is
  ;; no route from this module's source to those bytes. `/usr/bin/printf`
  ;; expands the octal escapes, and the redirect is the write.
  ;;
  ;; MEASURED 2026-08-16 against llmll 0.16.0: the argv member reaches printf as
  ;; backslash-3-7-7 and the file that lands is
  ;; `62 69 6e 61 72 79 20 ff fe 00 20 72 61 77 0a`, which is the subject's
  ;; `printf 'binary \377\376\000 raw\n'` byte for byte. The gap row is
  ;; `BYTES-WRITE-1`, and it is the same shape as `BYTES-READ-1`: nothing in the
  ;; language moves bytes that are not text.
  (def-shell fe-bin-fmt [] -> string "binary \\377\\376\\000 raw\\n")

  ;; The multi-buffer input, and the DIVERGENCE here is deliberate. The subject
  ;; builds 4000 numbered lines with `awk`; this port spawns no awk and builds
  ;; 4096 identical ones by doubling, exactly as ps-feed-text does. The reason
  ;; is a property of the fixture rather than a trick: fs_encoding.llmll reads
  ;; this file and writes it back, and it never inspects a line. So the CONTENT
  ;; is free and only two things are owed, both of which the subject's comment
  ;; names. It must be past GHC's 8192-byte handle buffer, because a short
  ;; string survives a force-after-close and proves nothing; 4096 lines of 40
  ;; bytes is 163840, MEASURED on disk and twenty buffers clear. And it must be
  ;; non-ASCII THROUGHOUT, so the round trip exercises the decoder and not
  ;; merely the byte count.
  (def-shell fe-big-line [] -> string "line section § padding padding padding\n")

  (def-shell fe-big-text [] -> string
    (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl (fe-big-line))))))))))))))

  ;; `sed -n 's/^src=//p'`, which is how the subject reads the two digits the
  ;; fixture prints. The fold keeps the LAST match where sed prints every one;
  ;; the fixture emits each key once, so the two agree on this input. An absent
  ;; key answers "", which is the emptiness the digests check tests for.
  (def-shell fe-field [out: string k: string] -> string
    (list-fold (string-split "\n" out) ""
      (fn [acc: string l: string]
        (if (= (string-slice l 0 (string-length k)) k)
            (string-slice l (string-length k) (string-length l))
            acc))))

  ;; U+00A7 is the character 45 files in this repository's own corpus carry.
  ;;
  ;; THIS ASSERTION IS A FIXED POINT OF THE BUG IT NAMES, and the port says so
  ;; rather than claiming more than it has. The needle is a literal in THIS
  ;; file, compared against text this file read back through the same UTF-8
  ;; pin. If the pin were missing on both sides they would fail identically and
  ;; the substring would still match. The subject has the same property, because
  ;; its needle is a literal in the shell script. What settles the encoding of a
  ;; literal end to end is stage 5c, which holds the HEX and not the characters.
  (def-shell fe-marker [] -> string "text=section § marker")
  (def-shell fe-marker? [out: string] -> bool (string-contains out (fe-marker)))

  (def-shell fe-digests? [out: string] -> bool
    (and (not (string-empty? (fe-field out "src=")))
         (not (string-empty? (fe-field out "dst=")))))

  (def-shell fe-faithful? [out: string] -> bool
    (= (fe-field out "src=") (fe-field out "dst=")))

  ;; The port's `cmp -s`. No builtin compares two files and none reads bytes
  ;; (`BYTES-READ-1`), so the digests do it, which is the substitution
  ;; TOOL-RFC-006 section 5 dispositions and section 9's D3 probed:
  ;; `wasi.fs.sha256` answers lowercase hex matching `shasum -a 256` byte for
  ;; byte.
  ;;
  ;; THE EMPTINESS TEST IS NOT DECORATION. A failed hash publishes RErr and
  ;; bs-text renders it as the message, so two ABSENT files would compare two
  ;; error strings rather than two digests. MEASURED: the message embeds the
  ;; PATH -- `fp_absent_one: withBinaryFile: does not exist (No such file or
  ;; directory)` -- so two absent files answer two DIFFERENT strings and cannot
  ;; agree by accident. The guard is kept anyway, because that is a property of
  ;; the message text and not of the contract.
  (def-shell fe-same? [a: string b: string] -> bool
    (and (not (string-empty? a)) (= a b)))

  ;; The subject's named failure reasons, one per assertion, collected the way
  ;; x-miss collects stage 5's: reporting only the first would make a two-defect
  ;; run look like a one-defect run.
  ;;
  ;; The third is guarded by the second, reproducing the subject's `elif`: a run
  ;; with NO digests has already reported that, and adding "src= dst=" under it
  ;; names a difference that is really an absence.
  ;;
  ;; THE FIFTH DIVERGES IN ITS TEXT AND NOT IN ITS VERDICT. The subject reports
  ;; the two files' byte counts, taken with `wc -c`. Nothing here answers a
  ;; file's size (`FS-STAT-1`, open), so the port names the two digests instead.
  ;; Both say which side is wrong; only the subject's says by how much.
  (def-shell fe-miss [out: string h1: string h2: string h3: string h4: string] -> list[string]
    (list-filter
      [(if (fe-marker? out) ""
           "the non-ASCII write/read round trip did not survive LC_ALL=C (FS-ENCODING-1)")
       (if (fe-digests? out) ""
           "one or both digests are missing; the copy or a sha256 published RErr")
       (if (or (not (fe-digests? out)) (fe-faithful? out)) ""
           (string-concat-many
             ["wasi.fs.copy is not byte-faithful: src=" (fe-field out "src=")
              " dst=" (fe-field out "dst=") " (FS-COPY-1)"]))
       (if (fe-same? h1 h2) ""
           "bin.copy does not match bin.dat on disk")
       (if (fe-same? h3 h4) ""
           (string-concat-many
             ["the multi-buffer read/write round trip did not preserve the file (big.txt "
              h3 ", big.copy " h4 "); the force may have escaped the bracket"]))]
      (fn [m: string] (not (string-empty? m)))))

  ;; The subject's second echo, verbatim, with `$(uname -s)` interpolated from
  ;; the same read that decided the verdict. One stdout and not two, because a
  ;; phase emits one Command; the bytes are the subject's two lines.
  (def-shell fe-not-exercised [un: string] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      [(pass-line "wasi.fs.copy byte-faithful; multi-buffer round trip preserved")
       "\nBUILD-GATE-1 NOT EXERCISED: the LC_ALL=C encoding claim (FS-ENCODING-1).\n  GHC on "
       un
       " resolves UTF-8 whatever LC_ALL says, so this run cannot\n  distinguish a working pin from a missing one. Only Linux CI settles it; do not\n  read this stage's green as evidence for the encoding half. See the note above."]))

  (def-shell fe-build [b: Bs] -> Command
    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                   ["build" (fe-fixture-of (get-s b "root")) "-o" (fe-out-of (get-s b "work"))]
                   (get-s b "root")
                   (fe-log-of (get-s b "work")) (fe-log-of (get-s b "work"))
                   900 "/dev/null"))

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; Stages 8, 9 and 10. All three have the SAME shape and it is the
  ;; simplest in the file: build an LLMLL program, ask stack where its binary
  ;; landed, hand that binary to a Python cover script, and read one exit
  ;; status. **The per-cell assertions are NOT here and are not ported**: the
  ;; subject delegates them to `scripts/driver_ll_cover.py`,
  ;; `scripts/wave_cover.py` and `scripts/version_gate_cover.py`, and it
  ;; grades each by exit status alone. A port that re-implemented the cells
  ;; would not be reproducing its reference; it would be replacing it.
  ;;
  ;; **The subject's `[ -f "$SRC" ]` guard is NOT reproduced, and that is a
  ;; divergence rather than an omission.** No builtin answers "is there a file
  ;; here" (`FS-EXISTS-1`), so a missing fixture reaches the port as a build
  ;; failure where the subject skips the stage in silence. All three fixtures
  ;; are committed, so the guard is true on every tree the gate runs against
  ;; and the divergence is unreachable here. It is recorded because an
  ;; unreachable divergence is still a divergence.
  (def-shell d-dir-of   [root: string] -> string (string-concat root "/tools/llmll-driver"))
  (def-shell d-out-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/driverll"))
  (def-shell d-log-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.driverll-build.log"))
  (def-shell d-sir-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.driverll-sir.txt"))
  (def-shell d-cov-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.driverll-cover.log"))
  (def-shell d-cover-py [root: string] -> string (string-concat root "/scripts/driver_ll_cover.py"))

  (def-shell w-out-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/waverun"))
  (def-shell w-log-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.wave-build.log"))
  (def-shell w-sir-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.wave-sir.txt"))
  (def-shell w-cov-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.wave-cover.log"))
  (def-shell w-cover-py [root: string] -> string (string-concat root "/scripts/wave_cover.py"))

  (def-shell v-dir-of   [root: string] -> string (string-concat root "/tools/version-gate"))
  (def-shell v-out-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/versiongate"))
  (def-shell v-log-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.versiongate-build.log"))
  (def-shell v-sir-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.versiongate-sir.txt"))
  (def-shell v-cov-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.versiongate-cover.log"))
  (def-shell v-live-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.versiongate-live.log"))
  (def-shell v-feed-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.versiongate-stdin.txt"))
  (def-shell v-cover-py [root: string] -> string (string-concat root "/scripts/version_gate_cover.py"))

  ;; The subject feeds the live gate sixty lines, and the count is the step
  ;; budget rather than data. It writes them with python3 and NOT with
  ;; `yes x | head -n 60`, because `yes` dies of SIGPIPE and the subject runs
  ;; under `set -o pipefail`. The port writes the same sixty lines directly,
  ;; so neither hazard applies to it.
  ;;
  ;; `range` IS HALF-OPEN, so `(range 1 61)` is sixty elements and not
  ;; sixty-one. That is measured against llmll 0.16.0 and not read off the
  ;; type: the same `list-map` over the sixty-element literal this call
  ;; replaced emits the same 120 bytes. The count is a step budget, so a list
  ;; one longer or one shorter would silently move it.
  (def-shell v-feed-text [] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      (list-map (range 1 61) (fn [n: int] "x\n"))))

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; Stages 5c, 5d and 5e: CAPTURE-ENCODING-1, JSON-SCALAR-1, PROC-MERGE-1.
  ;;
  ;; All three assert on WHOLE LINES, because the subject uses `grep -Fqx`.
  ;; `has-line?` is that predicate: a fixed string equal to an entire line.
  ;; `string-contains` would NOT reproduce it, and the difference is not
  ;; pedantic here: `X-AS-INT=err` is a substring of a line reporting
  ;; `X-AS-INT=errand`, and the whole point of those cells is that a mis-typed
  ;; read REFUSES rather than answering something adjacent.
  (def-shell has-line? [out: string want: string] -> bool
    (list-contains (string-split "\n" out) want))

  ;; `tr -d ' \n'` over an `od` dump. Two passes, because string-split takes
  ;; one separator: drop the line ends, then drop the spaces.
  (def-shell strip-ws [s: string] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      (string-split " " (string-concat-many (string-split "\n" s)))))

  (def-shell c-out-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/capenc"))
  (def-shell c-log-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.capenc-build.log"))
  (def-shell c-sir-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.capenc-sir.txt"))
  (def-shell c-feed-of [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.capenc-stdin.txt"))
  (def-shell c-run-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.capenc.out"))
  (def-shell c-hex-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.capenc.hex"))
  (def-shell c-fixture-of [root: string] -> string
    (string-concat root "/scripts/build-smoke/capture_encoding.llmll"))
  ;; UTF-8 for `BMP=...|AST=...|LEN=5|ALEN=1`. The port holds the HEX and not
  ;; the characters: a source file carrying them would be checking that THIS
  ;; file survived its own encoding, not that the fixture's output did.
  (def-shell c-want [] -> string
    "424d503de28692e29c94e29c98e29c85c2a77c4153543df09f98807c4c454e3d357c414c454e3d31")

  (def-shell j-out-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/jsonscalar"))
  (def-shell j-log-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.jsonscalar-build.log"))
  (def-shell j-sir-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.jsonscalar-sir.txt"))
  (def-shell j-feed-of [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.jsonscalar-stdin.txt"))
  (def-shell j-run-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.jsonscalar.out"))
  (def-shell j-fixture-of [root: string] -> string
    (string-concat root "/scripts/build-smoke/json_scalar.llmll"))
  (def-shell j-want [] -> string
    "STR=--strict-verified-core|INT=7|BOOL=true|NUM=1.5|X-AS-INT=err|N-AS-STR=err|OLD-GET=err")

  (def-shell p-out-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/procmerge"))
  (def-shell p-log-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.procmerge-build.log"))
  (def-shell p-sir-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.procmerge-sir.txt"))
  (def-shell p-feed-of [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.procmerge-stdin.txt"))
  (def-shell p-run-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.procmerge.out"))
  (def-shell p-fixture-of [root: string] -> string
    (string-concat root "/scripts/build-smoke/proc_merge.llmll"))

  ;; One stdin line per step. The counts are the subject's own step budgets.
  (def-shell c-feed-text [] -> string "x\nx\nx\nx\nx\nx\n")
  (def-shell j-feed-text [] -> string "x\nx\nx\nx\n")
  (def-shell p-feed-text [] -> string "x\nx\nx\nx\nx\nx\nx\nx\n")

  (def-shell cenc-build [b: Bs] -> Command
    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                   ["build" (c-fixture-of (get-s b "root")) "-o" (c-out-of (get-s b "work"))]
                   (get-s b "root")
                   (c-log-of (get-s b "work")) (c-log-of (get-s b "work"))
                   900 "/dev/null"))

  (def-shell js-build [b: Bs] -> Command
    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                   ["build" (j-fixture-of (get-s b "root")) "-o" (j-out-of (get-s b "work"))]
                   (get-s b "root")
                   (j-log-of (get-s b "work")) (j-log-of (get-s b "work"))
                   900 "/dev/null"))

  (def-shell pm-build [b: Bs] -> Command
    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                   ["build" (p-fixture-of (get-s b "root")) "-o" (p-out-of (get-s b "work"))]
                   (get-s b "root")
                   (p-log-of (get-s b "work")) (p-log-of (get-s b "work"))
                   900 "/dev/null"))

  ;; Each of the three builds runs from the FIXTURE's own directory with a
  ;; RELATIVE source argument, exactly as the subject does. That is not a
  ;; style choice: sibling-module imports resolve relative to the source file,
  ;; so an absolute path from another cwd does not find them.
  (def-shell drv8-build [b: Bs] -> Command
    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                   ["build" "sequencer.llmll" "-o" (d-out-of (get-s b "work"))]
                   (d-dir-of (get-s b "root"))
                   (d-log-of (get-s b "work"))
                   (d-log-of (get-s b "work"))
                   900 "/dev/null"))

  (def-shell wav9-build [b: Bs] -> Command
    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                   ["build" "wave.llmll" "-o" (w-out-of (get-s b "work"))]
                   (d-dir-of (get-s b "root"))
                   (w-log-of (get-s b "work"))
                   (w-log-of (get-s b "work"))
                   900 "/dev/null"))

  (def-shell vg10-build [b: Bs] -> Command
    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                   ["build" "versiongate.llmll" "-o" (v-out-of (get-s b "work"))]
                   (v-dir-of (get-s b "root"))
                   (v-log-of (get-s b "work"))
                   (v-log-of (get-s b "work"))
                   900 "/dev/null"))

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; PROC-STDIN-1. The subject's UNNUMBERED stage, build_smoke.sh:686-739.
  ;;
  ;; THE STDIN VOLUME IS THE WHOLE TEST, and the subject says so in capitals.
  ;; Below the 8192-byte handle buffer the sharing defect is invisible. The
  ;; subject measured it: a child read NOTHING at 7893 bytes of parent stdin,
  ;; and read "NE1034", the torn tail of the parent's own "LINE1034", at 9693.
  ;;
  ;; THE SUBJECT BUILDS ITS VOLUME WITH `printf 'LINE%s\n' $(seq 12000)`,
  ;; which is 108894 bytes, MEASURED. This port spawns neither seq nor awk,
  ;; and the reason is a property of the fixture rather than a trick.
  ;; proc_stdin.llmll NEVER INSPECTS ITS OWN STEP INPUT; it counts steps. So
  ;; the line CONTENT is free and only the byte count is owed. Fourteen
  ;; doublings of a 9-byte line give 16384 lines and 147456 bytes, which is
  ;; ABOVE the subject's figure, so the subject's "do not lower it" holds.
  ;;
  ;; TOOL-RFC-006 PREDICTED an `awk BEGIN` workaround at this exact site and
  ;; filed LIST-RANGE-1 against it. THAT PREDICTION IS WITHDRAWN, because it
  ;; assumed the fixture needed the bytes LINE1 through LINE12000 and the
  ;; fixture reads none of them. The awk one never existed. The prediction
  ;; stays written down: this file records what it got wrong, not only the
  ;; corrected claim.
  ;;
  ;; **LIST-RANGE-1 IS REFUTED OUTRIGHT AND IS BEING CLOSED IN THE ROADMAP.**
  ;; The row's premise was that nothing makes a list from a count. `range`
  ;; does. It is in builtinEnv typed `[int, int] -> list[int]`, it has a real
  ;; body in CodegenHs (`range from to = [from .. to - 1]`), and it has shipped
  ;; since v0.11, so it answered the row before the row was filed. The one
  ;; witness the row was left open on was v-feed-text's sixty-element literal
  ;; above; that site now reads `(range 1 61)`, measured to emit the identical
  ;; bytes, and no literal count-list remains in this file.
  (def-shell dbl [s: string] -> string (string-concat s s))

  (def-shell ps-feed-text [] -> string
    (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl
      (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl (dbl "LINE0001\n")))))))))))))))

  (def-shell ps-out-of     [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/procstdin"))
  (def-shell ps-log-of     [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.procstdin-build.log"))
  (def-shell ps-sir-of     [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.procstdin-sir.txt"))
  (def-shell ps-feed-of    [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.procstdin-stdin.txt"))
  (def-shell ps-run-of     [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.procstdin.out"))
  (def-shell ps-rundir-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.procstdin-run"))
  (def-shell ps-fixture-of [root: string] -> string
    (string-concat root "/scripts/build-smoke/proc_stdin.llmll"))

  ;; Two halves that fail in OPPOSITE directions, and both are required. FED
  ;; asserts a child reads the file the program named, which is the feature.
  ;; EMPTY asserts a child given "/dev/null" reads nothing while the parent
  ;; holds 147456 bytes, which is the defect. A build that CLOSES descriptor
  ;; 0 rather than binding it passes EMPTY and fails FED, so dropping either
  ;; assertion admits a wrong build.
  (def-shell ps-want-fed  [] -> string "FED|FED=y|EMPTY=n")
  (def-shell ps-want-null [] -> string "NULL|FED=n|EMPTY=y")

  (def-shell ps-build [b: Bs] -> Command
    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                   ["build" (ps-fixture-of (get-s b "root")) "-o" (ps-out-of (get-s b "work"))]
                   (get-s b "root")
                   (ps-log-of (get-s b "work")) (ps-log-of (get-s b "work"))
                   900 "/dev/null"))

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; Stage 6, REPLAY-FRAME. build_smoke.sh:741-847.
  ;;
  ;; ONE CHAIN, ENTERED TWICE. The subject calls a replay_case function twice.
  ;; A Ctl phase cannot call a subroutine that spans steps, so the index sits
  ;; in the record under "rpidx" and the last phase re-enters. The resolved
  ;; strings are written into the record ONCE at entry rather than recomputed
  ;; in each phase, so no phase carries a two-way branch and the two fixtures
  ;; cannot drift apart.
  ;;
  ;; Index 0 is replay-demo, the SHIPPED example with no :done? and no :init.
  ;; Index 1 is replay_settle, which declares both. The order is the
  ;; subject's, and stage 7 below consumes the replay-demo binary that index
  ;; 0 builds.
  (def-shell rp-dir-of [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/replay"))
  (def-shell rp-mod-of [i: string] -> string
    (if (= i "0") "replay-demo" "replay_settle"))
  ;; BUG-2 (v0.14.3): the on-disk executable name is the hpack-sanitized
  ;; package name, so an underscore becomes a hyphen. Stage 5 above hardcodes
  ;; capproc-exec for the same reason, and the subject hardcodes it too.
  (def-shell rp-exe-of [i: string] -> string
    (if (= i "0") "replay-demo" "replay-settle"))
  (def-shell rp-src-of [root: string i: string] -> string
    (if (= i "0")
        (string-concat root "/examples/replay-demo/replay-demo.llmll")
        (string-concat root "/scripts/build-smoke/replay_settle.llmll")))

  (def-shell rp-at [work: string i: string suffix: string] -> string
    (string-concat-many [(rp-dir-of work) "/" (rp-mod-of i) suffix]))
  (def-shell rp-llmll-of [work: string i: string] -> string (rp-at work i ".llmll"))
  (def-shell rp-elog-of  [work: string i: string] -> string (rp-at work i ".event-log.jsonl"))
  (def-shell rp-tam-of   [work: string i: string] -> string (rp-at work i ".tampered.jsonl"))
  (def-shell rp-bl-of    [work: string i: string] -> string (rp-at work i ".build.log"))
  (def-shell rp-sir-of   [work: string i: string] -> string (rp-at work i ".sir.txt"))
  (def-shell rp-run-of   [work: string i: string] -> string (rp-at work i ".run.out"))
  (def-shell rp-rep-of   [work: string i: string] -> string (rp-at work i ".replay.out"))
  (def-shell rp-trep-of  [work: string i: string] -> string (rp-at work i ".treplay.out"))
  (def-shell rp-warn-of  [work: string i: string] -> string (rp-at work i ".warn.out"))
  (def-shell rp-feed-of  [work: string i: string] -> string (rp-at work i ".stdin.txt"))
  (def-shell rp-gen-of   [work: string i: string] -> string
    (string-concat-many [(rp-dir-of work) "/generated/" (rp-mod-of i)]))

  (def-shell rp-feed-text [] -> string "x\nx\n")

  ;; The chain's single entry point, used at index 0 and again at index 1. One
  ;; definition rather than two copies, because a second copy is where the two
  ;; fixtures would drift apart.
  (def-shell rp-enter [b: Bs i: string] -> ((Bs, Ctl), Command)
    (go (with-s b "rpidx" i) RpCp
        (wasi.fs.copy (rp-src-of (get-s b "root") i)
                      (rp-llmll-of (get-s b "work") i))))

  ;; A SUBSTRING and NOT a whole line. The subject globs *"2/2 events
  ;; matched"* at build_smoke.sh:792, because the emitter puts the count on a
  ;; line with other text (Main.hs prints it beside the executable path it
  ;; ran). has-line? is the house predicate in stages 5c to 5e and it is the
  ;; WRONG one here: it would reject a correct run.
  (def-shell rp-want [] -> string "2/2 events matched")
  (def-shell rp-wi   [] -> string "W-REPLAY-INIT")

  ;; `sed 's/"kind":"stdout","value":"/&TAMPERED-/g'` at build_smoke.sh:806.
  ;; Replace-all COMPOSES from string-split and a join, so no builtin is owed
  ;; and no gap row is filed. The campaign measured this when it filed
  ;; LIST-RANGE-1 and BYTES-READ-1 and deliberately did not file a third.
  (def-shell rp-needle [] -> string "\"kind\":\"stdout\",\"value\":\"")

  (def-shell join-with [sep: string ps: list[string]] -> string
    (first (list-fold ps (pair "" false)
             (fn [acc: (string, bool) p: string]
               (pair (if (second acc) (string-concat-many [(first acc) sep p]) p)
                     true)))))

  (def-shell tamper [log: string] -> string
    (join-with (string-concat (rp-needle) "TAMPERED-")
               (string-split (rp-needle) log)))

  ;; wasi.fs.copy has NO idempotence guard: copyFile throws when the source is
  ;; missing OR when the destination directory is absent, and llmll_publish_io
  ;; turns that into RErr. So the mkdir runs BEFORE the copy, and the copy's
  ;; response is the one filesystem response this port checks. Every other
  ;; filesystem response here is discarded, exactly as the subject discards
  ;; the status of its own cp, mkdir -p and rm -f.
  (def-shell not-err? [r: Response] -> bool
    (match r
      ((RErr _)   false)
      ((RNone)    true)
      ((RCode _)  true)
      ((RText _)  true)
      ((RList _)  true)))

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; Stage 7, PROC-BOUNDARY-1. build_smoke.sh:849-969.
  ;;
  ;; TWO OF THE PROPERTIES ARE EXIT CODES, which a program cannot observe
  ;; about itself. wasi.proc.run answers the child's status as RCode, so this
  ;; port reads them directly and the subject's own note applies here in
  ;; translated form: the `|| true` idiom stages 5 and 6 use to swallow an
  ;; exit code CANNOT be used, because the exit code IS the observation.
  ;;
  ;; rc-is? and NOT ok?. ok? answers false for every non-zero code and for
  ;; RErr alike, and this stage needs 42 told apart from 70 told apart from a
  ;; timeout.
  (def-shell rc-is? [r: Response n: int] -> bool
    (match r
      ((RCode c)  (= c n))
      ((RText _)  false)
      ((RList _)  false)
      ((RNone)    false)
      ((RErr _)   false)))

  (def-shell pb-out-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/procboundary"))
  (def-shell pb-log-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.pb-build.log"))
  (def-shell pb-sir-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.pb-sir.txt"))
  (def-shell pb-f2-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.pb-2line.txt"))
  (def-shell pb-f1-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.pb-1line.txt"))
  (def-shell pb-a3-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.pb-argv3.out"))
  (def-shell pb-a0-of   [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.pb-argv0.out"))
  (def-shell pb-any-of  [work: string] -> string (string-concat work "/.pb-discard.out"))
  (def-shell pb-elog-of [work: string] -> string
    (string-concat (pb-out-of work) "/proc_boundary.event-log.jsonl"))
  (def-shell pb-fixture-of [root: string] -> string
    (string-concat root "/scripts/build-smoke/proc_boundary.llmll"))

  (def-shell pb-build [b: Bs] -> Command
    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                   ["build" (pb-fixture-of (get-s b "root")) "-o" (pb-out-of (get-s b "work"))]
                   (get-s b "root")
                   (pb-log-of (get-s b "work")) (pb-log-of (get-s b "work"))
                   900 "/dev/null"))

  (def-shell bs-init [] -> ((Bs, Ctl), Command)
    (go (bs-mk json-object) Boot wasi.proc.args))

  (def-shell bs-step [s: (Bs, Ctl) input: string r: Response] -> ((Bs, Ctl), Command)
    (match s
      ((pair b p)
        (match p
          ;; argv arrived. Record the contract's three values, then stage 1.
          ((Boot)
            (let [(as (argv-of r))]
              (go (with-s (with-s (with-s b "subject" (flag-or as "--subject" "llmll"))
                                  "root" (flag-or as "--root" "."))
                          "work" (flag-or as "--work" "."))
                  Tool1
                  (wasi.proc.run "stack" ["--version"] "."
                                 "bs_stack.txt" "bs_stack.txt" 60 "/dev/null"))))
          ;; stack answered. If it is absent, the subject still accepts ghc, so
          ;; a missing stack is not yet a failure.
          ((Tool1)
            (if (absent? r)
                (go b Tool1Ghc
                    (wasi.proc.run "ghc" ["--version"] "."
                                   "bs_ghc.txt" "bs_ghc.txt" 60 "/dev/null"))
                (go b Comp2
                    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject") ["version"] "."
                                   "bs_ver.txt" "bs_ver.txt" 60 "/dev/null"))))
          ;; ghc answered, and it is the last chance for stage 1.
          ((Tool1Ghc)
            (if (absent? r)
                (go b Ending (wasi.io.stdout (tool-fail)))
                (go b Comp2
                    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject") ["version"] "."
                                   "bs_ver.txt" "bs_ver.txt" 60 "/dev/null"))))
          ;; stage 2. The compiler answered, or it did not exist.
          ;; A compiler that did not answer is NOT yet a failure: the subject
          ;; has a third source and so does this port now. Falling straight to
          ;; comp-fail here is what dropped the branch before v0.15.0.
          ((Comp2)
            (if (absent? r)
                (go b Home2 (wasi.env.get "HOME"))
                ;; stage 2a fires only for the stack-shaped subject. A subject
                ;; that is already a path is already absolute enough: it ran.
                (if (stack-shaped? (get-s b "subject"))
                    (go b Anchor2a
                        (wasi.proc.run "stack" ["path" "--local-install-root"] "."
                                       "bs_sir.txt" "bs_sir.txt" 120 "/dev/null"))
                    (start-build b))))
          ;; stage 2, third source: $HOME arrived. Form the same path the
          ;; reference forms and try it. The subject becomes that path, so a
          ;; later stage runs what this phase resolved rather than the name the
          ;; caller passed and this port already found wanting.
          ((Home2)
            (let [(p (home-bin (home-of r)))]
              (go (with-s (with-s b "subject" p)
                          "homeunset" (if (home-unset? r) "yes" "no"))
                  Comp2b
                  (wasi.proc.run p ["version"] "."
                                 "bs_ver.txt" "bs_ver.txt" 60 "/dev/null"))))
          ;; stage 2, third source answered or it did not. This is the last
          ;; source, so absence IS the failure here.
          ;;
          ;; The success path calls start-build directly and does NOT re-test
          ;; stack-shaped?. The third source is an absolute path by
          ;; construction, so that branch could never fire and a copy of it
          ;; would be a dead arm a later edit could get wrong.
          ((Comp2b)
            (if (absent? r)
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (comp3-fail (get-s b "subject")
                                                (= (get-s b "homeunset") "yes"))))
                (start-build b)))
          ;; stage 2a, first source: an install root for the working directory.
          ;; BRANCH ON THE STATUS BEFORE READING THE ARTIFACT. This is the
          ;; header's ordering rule, and it is the defect the RFC's own first
          ;; probe shipped: reading bs_sir.txt without consulting the status
          ;; would take a STALE root from a previous run as this run's answer.
          ((Anchor2a)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b Anchor2aR (wasi.fs.read "bs_sir.txt"))
                (go b Anchor2aC
                    (wasi.proc.run "stack" ["path" "--local-install-root"]
                                   (string-concat (get-s b "root") "/compiler")
                                   "bs_sir2.txt" "bs_sir2.txt" 120 "/dev/null"))))
          ((Anchor2aR)
            (let [(sir (chomp (bs-text r)))]
              (if (string-empty? sir)
                  (go b Anchor2aC
                      (wasi.proc.run "stack" ["path" "--local-install-root"]
                                     (string-concat (get-s b "root") "/compiler")
                                     "bs_sir2.txt" "bs_sir2.txt" 120 "/dev/null"))
                  (go (with-s b "subject" (bin-under sir)) Anchor2aX1
                      (wasi.proc.run (bin-under sir) ["version"] "."
                                     "bs_anc.txt" "bs_anc.txt" 60 "/dev/null")))))
          ;; stage 2a, second source: the repository's own compiler project.
          ((Anchor2aC)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b Anchor2aCR (wasi.fs.read "bs_sir2.txt"))
                (go b Ending (wasi.io.stdout (anchor-fail)))))
          ((Anchor2aCR)
            (let [(sir (chomp (bs-text r)))]
              (if (string-empty? sir)
                  (go b Ending (wasi.io.stdout (anchor-fail)))
                  (go (with-s b "subject" (bin-under sir)) Anchor2aX2
                      (wasi.proc.run (bin-under sir) ["version"] "."
                                     "bs_anc.txt" "bs_anc.txt" 60 "/dev/null")))))
          ;; stage 2a, the checks that make the resolution a measurement. The
          ;; subject tests `-x` on the path; D3 established that attempting the
          ;; run answers the same question and says WHICH way it failed.
          ;;
          ;; The first source failing is NOT the stage failing. `stack path`
          ;; answers for whatever project the working directory belongs to, and
          ;; in CI that is the scratch tree, which has no compiler under its
          ;; root. The subject falls through to <root>/compiler for exactly
          ;; this case.
          ((Anchor2aX1)
            (if (or (absent? r) (denied? r))
                (go b Anchor2aC
                    (wasi.proc.run "stack" ["path" "--local-install-root"]
                                   (string-concat (get-s b "root") "/compiler")
                                   "bs_sir2.txt" "bs_sir2.txt" 120 "/dev/null"))
                (start-build b)))
          ((Anchor2aX2)
            (if (or (absent? r) (denied? r))
                (go b Ending (wasi.io.stdout (anchor-fail)))
                (start-build b)))
          ;; stage 3. THE STATUS IS CONSULTED HERE AND NOWHERE LATER. This is
          ;; the branch the RFC's first probe did not write, and without it the
          ;; stale src/Lib.hs from a previous run satisfies stage 4.
          ((Build3)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b Lib4 (wasi.fs.read (lib-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      (string-concat-many
                        ["the fixture does not build (status "
                         (bs-text r)
                         "). This is the defect class the gate exists for: it passes 'llmll check' and fails at GHC. Log: "
                         (log-of (get-s b "work"))]))))))
          ;; stage 4, first half: the emitted preamble must DEFINE every name
          ;; the fixture can call. A read error is the subject's "no src/Lib.hs
          ;; emitted" branch.
          ((Lib4)
            (let [(lib (bs-text r))]
              (if (string-empty? lib)
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                        (string-concat "no src/Lib.hs emitted at " (lib-of (get-s b "work"))))))
                  (let [(miss (missing-in lib))]
                    (if (= (list-length miss) 0)
                        (go b Log4 (wasi.fs.read (log-of (get-s b "work"))))
                        (go b Ending
                            (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                              (string-concat-many
                                ["src/Lib.hs is missing a definition for: "
                                 (string-concat-many (list-map miss (fn [n: string] (string-concat n " "))))
                                 ". The build reported success, which means it did not reach GHC (runGhcCheck's fail-open path) or the preamble drifted from builtinEnv."])))))))))
          ;; stage 4, second half: a success with no GHC invocation in the log
          ;; is the fail-open path, and the subject refuses to report green on
          ;; it. Two spellings are accepted because the two backends differ.
          ((Log4)
            (if (ghc-ran? (bs-text r))
                ;; Stage 4 passed. The subject prints its PASS line here and
                ;; continues to 4b, so the port does the same: a stage that
                ;; swallowed its own verdict would make the log unreadable
                ;; against the reference's.
                (go b Build4b
                    (seq-commands
                      (wasi.io.stdout (pass-line "fixture compiled through GHC; 0 missing preamble definitions"))
                      (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                                     ["build" (rx-fixture-of (get-s b "root"))
                                      "-o" (rx-out-of (get-s b "work"))]
                                     (get-s b "root")
                                     (rx-log-of (get-s b "work"))
                                     (rx-log-of (get-s b "work"))
                                     900 "/dev/null")))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      "build reported success but the log shows no GHC invocation. Fail-open path taken; the gate refuses to report green on an uncompiled fixture.")))))
          ;; stage 4b. The status first, as everywhere. A missing fixture is
          ;; also a build failure here: the subject tests `-f` on the path, and
          ;; no builtin answers that (FS-EXISTS-1), so attempting the build
          ;; answers the same question. D3 established the substitution.
          ((Build4b)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b Lib4b (wasi.fs.read (rx-lib-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      (string-concat-many
                        ["the REGEX-LOWER-1 fixture does not build (status "
                         (bs-text r)
                         "). If GHC reports 'Variable not in scope: regex', then isOperator (Parser.hs) is naming 'regex-match' again and emitOp's fallback is emitting it infix. That call must parse as an EApp so emitApp's default applies toHsIdent and binds the preamble's regex_match."]))))))
          ((Lib4b)
            (let [(lib (bs-text r))]
              (if (string-empty? lib)
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                        (string-concat "REGEX-LOWER-1: no src/Lib.hs emitted at " (rx-lib-of (get-s b "work"))))))
                  (if (not (rx-applied? lib))
                      (go b Ending
                          (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                            "REGEX-LOWER-1: src/Lib.hs has no prefix application of regex_match. The preamble defines it; if nothing calls it the builtin is dead again, which is the state this gate exists to detect.")))
                      (if (rx-hyphen? lib)
                          (go b Ending
                              (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                                "REGEX-LOWER-1: src/Lib.hs contains the hyphenated 'regex-match'. GHC lexes that hyphen as subtraction. isOperator must not name anything emitOp cannot lower; the call must reach emitApp and be mangled by toHsIdent.")))
                          (go b Build5
                              (seq-commands
                                (wasi.io.stdout (pass-line "REGEX-LOWER-1 fixture compiled and binds regex_match prefix"))
                                (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                                               ["build" (x-fixture-of (get-s b "root"))
                                                "-o" (x-out-of (get-s b "work"))]
                                               (get-s b "root")
                                               (x-log-of (get-s b "work"))
                                               (x-log-of (get-s b "work"))
                                               900 "/dev/null"))))))))
          ;; stage 5, the build.
          ((Build5)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b Sir5
                    (wasi.proc.run "stack" ["path" "--local-install-root"]
                                   (x-out-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   (x-sir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   (x-sir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   120 "/dev/null"))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      (string-concat "the execution fixture does not build (status "
                                     (string-concat (bs-text r) ")")))))))
          ;; stage 5, exe_path. Ask stack; do not search. The subject's own
          ;; helper does this and its comment says why: a tree can hold more
          ;; than one install root, readdir order is unspecified and a sorted
          ;; pick is deterministically stale.
          ((Sir5)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b Sir5R (wasi.fs.read (x-sir-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      "built the execution fixture but stack reported no install root for it. Without running it this stage observes nothing, which is the failure mode it exists to prevent.")))))
          ((Sir5R)
            (let [(sir (chomp (bs-text r)))]
              (if (string-empty? sir)
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                        "built the execution fixture but found no capproc-exec binary. Without running it this stage observes nothing, which is the failure mode it exists to prevent.")))
                  (go (with-s b "exe" (string-concat sir "/bin/capproc-exec"))
                      Bin5
                      (wasi.fs.list (string-concat sir "/bin"))))))
          ;; A NON-EMPTY INSTALL ROOT IS NOT A BINARY, and this port asserted
          ;; that it was until 2026-08-16. The subject's `exe_path` makes two
          ;; checks and this made one: it took `stack path` answering at all as
          ;; proof that the executable was under it.
          ;;
          ;; COVER CELL 6 IS THE WITNESS and it is why the check is here rather
          ;; than argued for. That cell lets the build SUCCEED, removes the
          ;; binary, and plants a working stale one elsewhere. The subject
          ;; refuses at exe_path and names the missing binary. This port ran the
          ;; absent path, read an empty capture, and reported all eight stage-5
          ;; behaviour assertions as failures: a real defect described as eight
          ;; imaginary ones, which is worse than the silence it replaced because
          ;; it names wasi.fs.sha256, wasi.proc.run and json-parse as broken
          ;; when nothing had run at all. Both sides failed, so an exit-code
          ;; comparison graded it green; the cover compares the CAUSE, which is
          ;; the whole reason it compares verdict text.
          ;;
          ;; The message is the empty-root branch's, verbatim, because the
          ;; subject reaches ONE `fail` from both of exe_path's two notes.
          ((Bin5)
            (if (has-entry? r "capproc-exec")
                (go b Feed5
                    (wasi.fs.write (x-feed-of (get-s b "work")) (x-feed-text)))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      "built the execution fixture but found no capproc-exec binary. Without running it this stage observes nothing, which is the failure mode it exists to prevent.")))))
          ;; stage 5, the scratch directory BEFORE the run.
          ;;
          ;; THE SUBJECT DELETES THIS DIRECTORY FIRST AND THE PORT NOW DOES TOO.
          ;; `FS-RMDIR-1` shipped `wasi.fs.rmdir`, so the clear the subject gets
          ;; from `rm -rf` is reproducible here. Without the clear, a directory
          ;; left by an earlier run satisfies the mkdir assertion whatever this
          ;; run's mkdir did, which is the stale-artifact class this file's
          ;; header is about.
          ;;
          ;; `wasi.fs.rmdir` is EMPTY-ONLY, so the three files the fixture
          ;; writes come off first. Each delete is idempotent, so a first run
          ;; with nothing there passes through them unchanged.
          ;;
          ;; THE NOT-GRADED PATH STAYS, and it is no longer about a missing
          ;; builtin. It now fires when the clear did not succeed: an unexpected
          ;; fourth entry leaves the directory non-empty, `wasi.fs.rmdir`
          ;; answers `RErr`, and `Pre5` still lists it. A gate that cannot
          ;; establish its own precondition must not report that it decided;
          ;; that is `SKIP-SILENT-1`, and it is the whole reason this phase
          ;; exists.
          ((Feed5)
            (go b Del5in  (wasi.fs.delete (string-concat (x-scratch) "/in.txt"))))
          ((Del5in)
            (go b Del5out (wasi.fs.delete (string-concat (x-scratch) "/out.txt"))))
          ((Del5out)
            (go b Del5err (wasi.fs.delete (string-concat (x-scratch) "/err.txt"))))
          ((Del5err)
            (go b Rmd5    (wasi.fs.rmdir (x-scratch))))
          ((Rmd5)
            (go b Pre5    (wasi.fs.list (x-scratch))))
          ((Pre5)
            (go (with-s b "pre" (if (listed? r) "y" "n")) Run5
                (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "exe") [] (get-s b "work")
                               (x-run-of (get-s b "work"))
                               (x-run-of (get-s b "work"))
                               300
                               (x-feed-of (get-s b "work")))))
          ;; The run's own exit status is NOT the assertion. The subject appends
          ;; `|| true` and grades the OUTPUT, because the fixture's console
          ;; harness exits 70 on end of input by design.
          ((Run5)
            (go b Out5 (wasi.fs.read (x-run-of (get-s b "work")))))
          ((Out5)
            (go (with-s b "out" (bs-text r)) Post5 (wasi.fs.list (x-scratch))))
          ((Post5)
            (let [(ms (x-miss (get-s b "out")))]
              (if (> (list-length ms) 0)
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                        (string-concat
                          "the CAP-PROC bodies compiled but did not behave. A body that links and does nothing is the WASI-RT defect class; this stage is its only oracle"
                          (x-report ms)))))
                  (if (not (listed? r))
                      (go b Ending
                          (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                            (string-concat "wasi.fs.mkdir did not create " (x-scratch)))))
                      (if (= (get-s b "pre") "y")
                          (go b Fe
                              (seq-commands
                                (wasi.io.stdout (string-concat-many
                                  ["BUILD-GATE-1 PASS: CAP-PROC operations executed and matched known answers"
                                   " (NOT GRADED: wasi.fs.mkdir. "
                                   (x-scratch)
                                   " still listed after the clear, so this run could not establish that mkdir created it)"]))
                                (fe-build b)))
                          (go b Fe
                              (seq-commands
                                (wasi.io.stdout (pass-line "CAP-PROC operations executed and matched known answers"))
                                (fe-build b))))))))
          ;; stage 5b, FS-ENCODING-1 and FS-COPY-1.
          ((Fe)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b FeS
                    (wasi.proc.run "stack" ["path" "--local-install-root"]
                                   (fe-out-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   (fe-sir-of (get-s b "work")) (fe-sir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   120 "/dev/null"))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "the fs-encoding fixture does not build.")))))
          ((FeS)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b FeR (wasi.fs.read (fe-sir-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "built the fs-encoding fixture but stack reported no install root for it.")))))
          ((FeR)
            (let [(sir (chomp (bs-text r)))]
              (if (string-empty? sir)
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "built the fs-encoding fixture but found no fs-encoding binary.")))
                  (go (with-s b "feexe" (string-concat sir "/bin/fs-encoding"))
                      FeD1
                      (wasi.fs.delete (fe-at "text.txt"))))))
          ;; The clear, and it is the SAME argument stage 5's makes: without it
          ;; a big.copy left by an earlier good run satisfies this run's
          ;; truncation guard whatever this run's fixture did, which is the
          ;; stale-artifact class this file's header is about. `wasi.fs.rmdir`
          ;; is empty-only, so the five files the fixture leaves come off first
          ;; and each delete is idempotent.
          ;;
          ;; NO PHASE HERE BRANCHES ON ITS RESPONSE, and that is a divergence
          ;; from Rmd5 above rather than an oversight. Stage 5 grades the clear
          ;; because the fixture's own mkdir is what it is testing. Here the
          ;; port supplies both inputs itself, so a clear that failed shows up
          ;; as the assertion on those inputs failing, and a `wasi.fs.delete`
          ;; RErr on a path that was already absent is the normal first run.
          ;;
          ;; THE CLEAR IS WEAKER THAN `rm -rf` AND THE DIFFERENCE IS REACHABLE.
          ;; `wasi.fs.delete` REFUSES a directory and `wasi.fs.rmdir` is
          ;; empty-only, so a directory standing where one of these five files
          ;; belongs survives every phase here and survives the next run too.
          ;; Nothing composes a recursive remove, which is the gap. MEASURED, by
          ;; the three negative controls this stage was graded with: each one
          ;; puts a directory at one of these paths and each one makes the stage
          ;; FAIL and name the assertion, which is the safe direction. It does
          ;; not clean up after itself, and the subject would have.
          ((FeD1) (go b FeD2 (wasi.fs.delete (fe-at "bin.dat"))))
          ((FeD2) (go b FeD3 (wasi.fs.delete (fe-at "bin.copy"))))
          ((FeD3) (go b FeD4 (wasi.fs.delete (fe-at "big.txt"))))
          ((FeD4) (go b FeD5 (wasi.fs.delete (fe-at "big.copy"))))
          ((FeD5) (go b FeRm (wasi.fs.rmdir (fe-scratch))))
          ((FeRm) (go b FeMk (wasi.fs.mkdir (fe-scratch))))
          ((FeMk)
            (go b FeBin
                (wasi.proc.run "/usr/bin/printf" [(fe-bin-fmt)]
                               (get-s b "work")
                               (fe-at "bin.dat") (fe-pf-of (get-s b "work"))
                               60 "/dev/null")))
          ;; The two output paths are DIFFERENT here and equal at every other
          ;; spawn in this file. PROC-MERGE-1 says equal paths merge, and a
          ;; merged stderr would be part of bin.dat: the adversarial input this
          ;; stage copies would then carry printf's diagnostics.
          ((FeBin)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b FeBig
                    (seq-commands
                      (wasi.fs.write (fe-at "big.txt") (fe-big-text))
                      (wasi.fs.write (fe-feed-of (get-s b "work")) (x-feed-text))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      (string-concat
                        "/usr/bin/printf could not write the binary input for the fs-encoding fixture. Its diagnostics: "
                        (fe-pf-of (get-s b "work"))))))))
          ;; THE PLATFORM READ, and it is a spawn because nothing in the
          ;; language answers it. `wasi.env.get` reads a variable, and no
          ;; variable carries the operating system; `uname -s` is what the
          ;; subject asks and this asks the same question of the same program.
          ;; Filed as `PLATFORM-1`, disposition SHAPES: it is one more exec on a
          ;; path this stage already takes, so it costs a phase pair and no
          ;; verdict, and a port that could not spawn could not reach it.
          ((FeBig)
            (go b FePlat
                (wasi.proc.run "/usr/bin/uname" ["-s"] (get-s b "work")
                               (fe-un-of (get-s b "work")) (fe-un-of (get-s b "work"))
                               60 "/dev/null")))
          ((FePlat)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b FeUn (wasi.fs.read (fe-un-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "uname did not answer, so this run cannot say which verdict stage 5b is entitled to print. Refusing to guess is the point: the guess that reads DARWIN as honoured is the over-claim that shipped in v0.14.84.")))))
          ;; Darwin means GHC does NOT honour LC_ALL; anything else means it
          ;; does. The subject's `case` is the same two arms. If GHC on macOS
          ;; ever starts honouring it, this UNDER-claims rather than
          ;; over-claims, which is the safe direction to rot in.
          ((FeUn)
            (let [(un (chomp (bs-text r)))]
              (go (with-s (with-s b "uname" un)
                          "honoured" (if (string-contains un "Darwin") "n" "y"))
                  FeRun
                  (bs-env ["LC_ALL=C"] (get-s b "feexe") []
                          (get-s b "work")
                          (fe-run-of (get-s b "work")) (fe-run-of (get-s b "work"))
                          300
                          (fe-feed-of (get-s b "work"))))))
          ;; The run's exit status is NOT the assertion, exactly as at Run5: the
          ;; subject appends `|| true` and grades the output, because the
          ;; fixture's console harness exits 70 on end of input by design.
          ((FeRun)
            (go b FeOut (wasi.fs.read (fe-run-of (get-s b "work")))))
          ((FeOut)
            (go (with-s b "feout" (bs-text r)) FeH1
                (wasi.fs.sha256 (fe-at "bin.dat"))))
          ((FeH1)
            (go (with-s b "feh1" (chomp (bs-text r))) FeH2
                (wasi.fs.sha256 (fe-at "bin.copy"))))
          ((FeH2)
            (go (with-s b "feh2" (chomp (bs-text r))) FeH3
                (wasi.fs.sha256 (fe-at "big.txt"))))
          ((FeH3)
            (go (with-s b "feh3" (chomp (bs-text r))) FeH4
                (wasi.fs.sha256 (fe-at "big.copy"))))
          ;; The verdict. fsenc-verdict is the PROVED half: it takes the six
          ;; booleans and answers 0, 1 or 2, and its contract pins the policy in
          ;; all three directions rather than the range alone. Everything above
          ;; it here is a recognizer and falls back.
          ((FeH4)
            (let [(out (get-s b "feout"))
                  (h1  (get-s b "feh1"))
                  (h2  (get-s b "feh2"))
                  (h3  (get-s b "feh3"))
                  (h4  (chomp (bs-text r)))
                  (un  (get-s b "uname"))]
              (let [(v (fsenc-verdict (fe-marker? out)
                                      (fe-digests? out)
                                      (fe-faithful? out)
                                      (fe-same? h1 h2)
                                      (fe-same? h3 h4)
                                      (= (get-s b "honoured") "y")))]
                (if (= v 0)
                    (go b Ending
                        (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                          (string-concat
                            "the fs bodies compiled but did not behave under a POSIX locale"
                            (x-report (fe-miss out h1 h2 h3 h4))))))
                    (if (= v 2)
                        (go b Cenc
                            (seq-commands
                              (wasi.io.stdout (pass-line "UTF-8 round trip survived LC_ALL=C; wasi.fs.copy byte-faithful"))
                              (cenc-build b)))
                        (go b Cenc
                            (seq-commands
                              (wasi.io.stdout (fe-not-exercised un))
                              (cenc-build b))))))))
          ;; stage 5c. The subject hex-dumps the program's stdout and compares
          ;; against a literal, because the defect it catches is invisible in
          ;; rendered text: a terminal shows the same glyphs whether the bytes
          ;; are UTF-8 or a latin-1 re-decode of them.
          ((Cenc)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b CencS
                    (wasi.proc.run "stack" ["path" "--local-install-root"]
                                   (c-out-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   (c-sir-of (get-s b "work")) (c-sir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   120 "/dev/null"))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "the capture-encoding fixture does not build.")))))
          ((CencS)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b CencR (wasi.fs.read (c-sir-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "built the capture-encoding fixture but stack reported no install root for it.")))))
          ((CencR)
            (let [(sir (chomp (bs-text r)))]
              (if (string-empty? sir)
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "built the capture-encoding fixture but found no capture-encoding binary.")))
                  (go (with-s b "cexe" (string-concat sir "/bin/capture-encoding"))
                      CencRun
                      (wasi.fs.write (c-feed-of (get-s b "work")) (c-feed-text))))))
          ((CencRun)
            (go b CencOd
                (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "cexe") [] (get-s b "work")
                               (c-run-of (get-s b "work")) (c-run-of (get-s b "work"))
                               300 (c-feed-of (get-s b "work")))))
          ((CencOd)
            (go b CencP
                (wasi.proc.run "od" ["-An" "-tx1" (c-run-of (get-s b "work"))]
                               (get-s b "work")
                               (c-hex-of (get-s b "work")) (c-hex-of (get-s b "work"))
                               120 "/dev/null")))
          ((CencP)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b Js (wasi.fs.read (c-hex-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "od could not dump the capture-encoding output.")))))
          ;; stage 5d.
          ((Js)
            (let [(hex (strip-ws (bs-text r)))]
              (if (string-contains hex (c-want))
                  (go b JsS
                      (seq-commands
                        (wasi.io.stdout (pass-line "non-ASCII string literals survive captureStdout as UTF-8 (5 BMP + 1 astral)"))
                        (js-build b)))
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                        (string-concat-many
                          ["a console program cannot put a non-ASCII character on its own stdout (CAPTURE-ENCODING-1). want (hex): "
                           (c-want) " got (hex): " hex])))))))
          ((JsS)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b JsR
                    (wasi.proc.run "stack" ["path" "--local-install-root"]
                                   (j-out-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   (j-sir-of (get-s b "work")) (j-sir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   120 "/dev/null"))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "the json-scalar fixture does not build.")))))
          ((JsR)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b JsRun (wasi.fs.read (j-sir-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "built the json-scalar fixture but stack reported no install root for it.")))))
          ((JsRun)
            (let [(sir (chomp (bs-text r)))]
              (if (string-empty? sir)
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "built the json-scalar fixture but found no json-scalar binary.")))
                  (go (with-s b "jexe" (string-concat sir "/bin/json-scalar"))
                      JsP
                      (wasi.fs.write (j-feed-of (get-s b "work")) (j-feed-text))))))
          ((JsP)
            (go b Pm
                (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "jexe") [] (get-s b "work")
                               (j-run-of (get-s b "work")) (j-run-of (get-s b "work"))
                               300 (j-feed-of (get-s b "work")))))
          ;; stage 5e.
          ((Pm)
            (go b PmS (wasi.fs.read (j-run-of (get-s b "work")))))
          ((PmS)
            (if (has-line? (bs-text r) (j-want))
                (go b PmR
                    (seq-commands
                      (wasi.io.stdout (pass-line "scalar json-array elements project to values, and mis-typed reads refuse"))
                      (pm-build b)))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      (string-concat-many
                        ["a scalar element of a json-array does not read back correctly (JSON-SCALAR-1). want: "
                         (j-want) " output: " (j-run-of (get-s b "work"))]))))))
          ((PmR)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b PmRun
                    (wasi.proc.run "stack" ["path" "--local-install-root"]
                                   (p-out-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   (p-sir-of (get-s b "work")) (p-sir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   120 "/dev/null"))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "the proc-merge fixture does not build.")))))
          ((PmRun)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b PmP (wasi.fs.read (p-sir-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "built the proc-merge fixture but stack reported no install root for it.")))))
          ((PmP)
            (let [(sir (chomp (bs-text r)))]
              (if (string-empty? sir)
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "built the proc-merge fixture but found no proc-merge binary.")))
                  (go (with-s b "pexe" (string-concat sir "/bin/proc-merge"))
                      PmDone
                      (wasi.fs.write (p-feed-of (get-s b "work")) (p-feed-text))))))
          ((PmDone)
            (go b PmOut
                (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "pexe") [] (get-s b "work")
                               (p-run-of (get-s b "work")) (p-run-of (get-s b "work"))
                               300 (p-feed-of (get-s b "work")))))
          ((PmOut)
            (go b PmFin (wasi.fs.read (p-run-of (get-s b "work")))))
          ;; BOTH lines are required and they are opposite claims: equal paths
          ;; MERGE, unequal paths STAY SEPARATE. Asserting only the first would
          ;; pass against an unconditional merge, which is the defect that
          ;; breaks every two-file caller.
          ((PmFin)
            (let [(out (bs-text r))]
              (if (and (has-line? out "MERGED|OUT=y|ERR=y")
                       (has-line? out "SPLIT-ERRFILE|OUT=n|ERR=y"))
                  (go b Ps
                      (seq-commands
                        (wasi.io.stdout (pass-line "equal proc.run paths merge both streams; unequal paths stay separate"))
                        (ps-build b)))
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                        (string-concat-many
                          ["wasi.proc.run's stream redirection does not match PROC-MERGE-1. want both: MERGED|OUT=y|ERR=y and SPLIT-ERRFILE|OUT=n|ERR=y. output: "
                           (p-run-of (get-s b "work"))])))))))
          ;; PROC-STDIN-1.
          ((Ps)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b PsS
                    (wasi.proc.run "stack" ["path" "--local-install-root"]
                                   (ps-out-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   (ps-sir-of (get-s b "work")) (ps-sir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   120 "/dev/null"))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "the proc-stdin fixture does not build.")))))
          ((PsS)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b PsR (wasi.fs.read (ps-sir-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "built the proc-stdin fixture but stack reported no install root for it.")))))
          ((PsR)
            (let [(sir (chomp (bs-text r)))]
              (if (string-empty? sir)
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "built the proc-stdin fixture but found no proc-stdin binary.")))
                  (go (with-s b "psexe" (string-concat sir "/bin/proc-stdin"))
                      PsRun
                      (seq-commands
                        (wasi.fs.mkdir (ps-rundir-of (get-s b "work")))
                        (wasi.fs.write (ps-feed-of (get-s b "work")) (ps-feed-text)))))))
          ;; The run's cwd is a scratch directory, because the fixture writes
          ;; ps_feed.txt beside wherever it stands. Its exit status is not
          ;; consulted, exactly as the subject's `|| true` does not consult it.
          ;; The two report lines are the observation.
          ((PsRun)
            (go b PsOut
                (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "psexe") [] (ps-rundir-of (get-s b "work"))
                               (ps-run-of (get-s b "work")) (ps-run-of (get-s b "work"))
                               300 (ps-feed-of (get-s b "work")))))
          ((PsOut)
            (go b PsFin (wasi.fs.read (ps-run-of (get-s b "work")))))
          ((PsFin)
            (let [(out (bs-text r))]
              (if (and (has-line? out (ps-want-fed))
                       (has-line? out (ps-want-null)))
                  (go b Rp
                      (seq-commands
                        (wasi.io.stdout (pass-line "a child reads its named stdin path, and a /dev/null child is not fed the parent's 147456 bytes"))
                        (wasi.fs.mkdir (rp-dir-of (get-s b "work")))))
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                        (string-concat-many
                          ["wasi.proc.run's stdin channel does not match PROC-STDIN-1. want BOTH: "
                           (ps-want-fed) " and " (ps-want-null) " program output: "
                           (ps-run-of (get-s b "work"))])))))))
          ;; stage 6, REPLAY-FRAME. Before this stage `llmll replay` was
          ;; executed by NO gate. Its only coverage was three unit tests driving
          ;; runReplay against bash mocks, and those mocks framed their output
          ;; differently from the emitter, so they passed while three divergence
          ;; classes shipped.
          ((Rp) (rp-enter b "0"))
          ((RpCp)
            (let [(i (get-s b "rpidx"))]
              (if (not-err? r)
                  (go b RpB
                      (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                                     ["build" (string-concat (rp-mod-of i) ".llmll")]
                                     (rp-dir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                     (rp-bl-of (get-s b "work") i) (rp-bl-of (get-s b "work") i)
                                     900 "/dev/null"))
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                        (string-concat "could not copy the replay fixture into the work tree: "
                                       (rp-src-of (get-s b "root") i))))))))
          ((RpB)
            (let [(i (get-s b "rpidx"))]
              (if (ok? r)
                  (go b RpS
                      (wasi.proc.run "stack" ["path" "--local-install-root"]
                                     (rp-gen-of (get-s b "work") i)
                                     (rp-sir-of (get-s b "work") i) (rp-sir-of (get-s b "work") i)
                                     120 "/dev/null"))
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                        (string-concat "replay fixture does not build: " (rp-mod-of i))))))))
          ((RpS)
            (let [(i (get-s b "rpidx"))]
              (if (ok? r)
                  (go b RpR (wasi.fs.read (rp-sir-of (get-s b "work") i)))
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                        (string-concat "built the replay fixture but stack reported no install root: "
                                       (rp-mod-of i))))))))
          ;; The replay-demo binary is saved under its OWN key as well, because
          ;; stage 7's no-:done? witness needs it after index 1 has overwritten
          ;; "rpexe". The subject rebuilds it there; this port keeps the one
          ;; stage 6 already built.
          ((RpR)
            (let [(i (get-s b "rpidx"))]
              (let [(sir (chomp (bs-text r)))]
                (if (string-empty? sir)
                    (go b Ending
                        (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                          (string-concat-many
                            ["built " (rp-mod-of i) " but found no executable named "
                             (rp-exe-of i) "; the replay stage would observe nothing"]))))
                    (let [(exe (string-concat-many [sir "/bin/" (rp-exe-of i)]))]
                      (go (if (= i "0")
                              (with-s (with-s b "rpexe" exe) "rdexe" exe)
                              (with-s b "rpexe" exe))
                          RpF
                          (wasi.fs.write (rp-feed-of (get-s b "work") i) (rp-feed-text))))))))
          ((RpF)
            (let [(i (get-s b "rpidx"))]
              (go b RpRun
                  (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "rpexe") [] (rp-dir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                 (rp-run-of (get-s b "work") i) (rp-run-of (get-s b "work") i)
                                 300 (rp-feed-of (get-s b "work") i)))))
          ((RpRun)
            (let [(i (get-s b "rpidx"))]
              (go b RpLog (wasi.fs.read (rp-elog-of (get-s b "work") i)))))
          ;; EQUAL stdout and stderr paths on EVERY replay call below, and that
          ;; is not cosmetic. The match line goes to stdout and the
          ;; W-REPLAY-INIT warning goes to stderr. Unequal paths would send the
          ;; two assertions to two different files, and the witness that must
          ;; NOT fire would then pass without reading anything.
          ((RpLog)
            (let [(i (get-s b "rpidx"))]
              (let [(log (bs-text r))]
                (if (not-err? r)
                    (go (with-s b "rplog" log) RpRep
                        (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                                       ["replay" (string-concat (rp-mod-of i) ".llmll")
                                                 (string-concat (rp-mod-of i) ".event-log.jsonl")]
                                       (rp-dir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                       (rp-rep-of (get-s b "work") i) (rp-rep-of (get-s b "work") i)
                                       300 "/dev/null"))
                    (go b Ending
                        (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                          (string-concat-many
                            [(rp-mod-of i) " produced no event log at "
                             (rp-elog-of (get-s b "work") i)]))))))))
          ((RpRep)
            (let [(i (get-s b "rpidx"))]
              (if (ok? r)
                  (go b RpOut (wasi.fs.read (rp-rep-of (get-s b "work") i)))
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                        (string-concat-many
                          [(rp-mod-of i) " replayed but exited non-zero. A recorded run that cannot be replayed is the defect class this stage exists for. output: "
                           (rp-rep-of (get-s b "work") i)])))))))
          ((RpOut)
            (let [(i (get-s b "rpidx"))]
              (if (string-contains (bs-text r) (rp-want))
                  (let [(tam (tamper (get-s b "rplog")))]
                    (if (= tam (get-s b "rplog"))
                        (go b Ending
                            (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                              (string-concat-many
                                ["the " (rp-mod-of i)
                                 " refute-crux did not perturb anything, so it proves nothing. Its needle no longer matches the log's recorded values"]))))
                        (go b RpTam
                            (wasi.fs.write (rp-tam-of (get-s b "work") i) tam))))
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                        (string-concat-many
                          [(rp-mod-of i) " did not replay " (rp-want) ". output: "
                           (rp-rep-of (get-s b "work") i)])))))))
          ((RpTam)
            (let [(i (get-s b "rpidx"))]
              (go b RpTRun
                  (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                                 ["replay" (string-concat (rp-mod-of i) ".llmll")
                                           (string-concat (rp-mod-of i) ".tampered.jsonl")]
                                 (rp-dir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                 (rp-trep-of (get-s b "work") i) (rp-trep-of (get-s b "work") i)
                                 300 "/dev/null"))))
          ;; A tampered log MUST fail. A replay oracle whose verdict never
          ;; reports divergence is indistinguishable from one that always
          ;; answers 2/2, which is the dead-gate mode this file refuses. The
          ;; assertion is on the EXIT CODE and not on the absence of a match
          ;; line: replay prints a count either way, and a tampered log simply
          ;; prints 0/2 before exiting non-zero.
          ((RpTRun)
            (let [(i (get-s b "rpidx"))]
              (if (ok? r)
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                        (string-concat-many
                          [(rp-mod-of i)
                           " replayed a TAMPERED log successfully. The oracle does not discriminate, so its verdict on the real log means nothing"]))))
                  (if (= i "0")
                      (rp-enter b "1")
                      (go b RpWi
                          (seq-commands
                            (wasi.io.stdout (pass-line "recorded runs replay clean and tampered logs are refuted"))
                            (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                                           ["replay" "replay_settle.llmll" "replay_settle.event-log.jsonl"]
                                           (rp-dir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                           (rp-warn-of (get-s b "work") "1")
                                           (rp-warn-of (get-s b "work") "1")
                                           300 "/dev/null")))))))
          ((RpWi)
            (go b RpWiP (wasi.fs.read (rp-warn-of (get-s b "work") "1"))))
          ((RpWiP)
            (if (string-contains (bs-text r) (rp-wi))
                (go b RpWd
                    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "subject")
                                   ["replay" "replay-demo.llmll" "replay-demo.event-log.jsonl"]
                                   (rp-dir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   (rp-warn-of (get-s b "work") "0")
                                   (rp-warn-of (get-s b "work") "0")
                                   300 "/dev/null"))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      "W-REPLAY-INIT did not fire on a program declaring :init. The warning exists because an :init that prints breaks replay alignment; if it never fires it warns nobody.")))))
          ((RpWd)
            (go b RpFin (wasi.fs.read (rp-warn-of (get-s b "work") "0"))))
          ((RpFin)
            (if (string-contains (bs-text r) (rp-wi))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      "W-REPLAY-INIT fired on replay-demo, which declares no :init. A warning that fires on every program is not a warning.")))
                (go b Pb
                    (seq-commands
                      (wasi.io.stdout (pass-line "W-REPLAY-INIT fires on an :init program and stays silent on one without"))
                      (pb-build b)))))
          ;; stage 7, PROC-BOUNDARY-1.
          ((Pb)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b PbS
                    (wasi.proc.run "stack" ["path" "--local-install-root"]
                                   (pb-out-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   (pb-sir-of (get-s b "work")) (pb-sir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   120 "/dev/null"))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "the process-boundary fixture does not build.")))))
          ((PbS)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b PbR (wasi.fs.read (pb-sir-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "built the process-boundary fixture but stack reported no install root for it.")))))
          ((PbR)
            (let [(sir (chomp (bs-text r)))]
              (if (string-empty? sir)
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "built the process-boundary fixture but found no proc-boundary binary. Without running it this stage observes nothing, which is the failure mode it exists to prevent.")))
                  (go (with-s b "pbexe" (string-concat sir "/bin/proc-boundary"))
                      PbF2
                      (wasi.fs.write (pb-f2-of (get-s b "work")) "x\nx\n")))))
          ((PbF2)
            (go b PbF1 (wasi.fs.write (pb-f1-of (get-s b "work")) "x\n")))
          ((PbF1)
            (go b PbA
                (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "pbexe") [] (get-s b "work")
                               (pb-any-of (get-s b "work")) (pb-any-of (get-s b "work"))
                               300 (pb-f2-of (get-s b "work")))))
          ;; (a) the :done? path. Two lines reach (>= s 2) and :status yields
          ;; 42, which is neither 0 nor 70 nor 1, so success, exhaustion and a
          ;; crash cannot produce it.
          ((PbA)
            (if (rc-is? r 42)
                (go b PbAr
                    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "pbexe") ["alpha" "beta" "gamma"] (get-s b "work")
                                   (pb-a3-of (get-s b "work")) (pb-a3-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   300 (pb-f2-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      (string-concat ":done? path did not exit with the 42 that :status returns; got "
                                     (bs-text r)))))))
          ((PbAr)
            (go b PbArP (wasi.fs.read (pb-a3-of (get-s b "work")))))
          ((PbArP)
            (if (string-contains (bs-text r) "argc=3")
                (go b PbA0
                    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "pbexe") [] (get-s b "work")
                                   (pb-a0-of (get-s b "work")) (pb-a0-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   300 (pb-f2-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "wasi.proc.args did not deliver three arguments through RC-3.")))))
          ;; The negative half. Without it a body publishing a constant
          ;; three-element list passes the assertion above.
          ((PbA0)
            (go b PbA0P (wasi.fs.read (pb-a0-of (get-s b "work")))))
          ((PbA0P)
            (if (string-contains (bs-text r) "argc=0")
                (go b PbSt
                    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "pbexe") [] (get-s b "work")
                                   (pb-any-of (get-s b "work")) (pb-any-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   300 (pb-f1-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "wasi.proc.args reported a non-empty vector for a no-argument run.")))))
          ;; (c) THE POSITIVE WITNESS. One line does not reach :done?, so stdin
          ;; is starved. Before PROC-BOUNDARY-1 this exited 0, with partial
          ;; state written and no diagnostic. It is the only assertion in this
          ;; file whose pre-change behaviour was SUCCESS.
          ((PbSt)
            (if (rc-is? r 70)
                (go b PbDel (wasi.fs.delete (pb-elog-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      (string-concat "a starved stdin did not exit 70, and it exited 0 before PROC-BOUNDARY-1; got "
                                     (bs-text r)))))))
          ;; The delete's response is DISCARDED, and that is deliberate rather
          ;; than lax. wasi.fs.delete is idempotent on a missing path and
          ;; answers RNone, so a clean tree arrives here with nothing to remove.
          ;; Gating on ok? would fail the stage on exactly that tree, because
          ;; ok? matches RCode 0 and RNone is not a code.
          ((PbDel)
            (go b PbEof
                (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "pbexe") [] (pb-out-of (get-s b "work"))
                               (pb-any-of (get-s b "work")) (pb-any-of (get-s b "work"))
                               300 "/dev/null")))
          ((PbEof)
            (if (rc-is? r 70)
                (go b PbHdr (wasi.fs.read (pb-elog-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      (string-concat "an immediate EOF did not exit 70; got " (bs-text r)))))))
          ;; The header is the ONLY log write with no following flush, so an
          ;; exit that jumped over hClose would lose it entirely.
          ((PbHdr)
            (if (string-contains (bs-text r) "\"type\":\"header\"")
                (go b PbRd
                    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "rdexe") [] (get-s b "work")
                                   (pb-any-of (get-s b "work")) (pb-any-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   300 (pb-f2-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "a zero-input run left no header in the event log; the exit jumped over hClose.")))))
          ;; THE OTHER HALF OF THE RULE, and it needs a SHIPPED program rather
          ;; than a purpose-built fixture. examples/replay-demo declares no
          ;; :done?, so EOF is the normal end of its input and it must exit 0.
          ;; The claim is a no-regression claim about the existing corpus, so
          ;; only a program that predates the change can make it. Stage 6 built
          ;; this binary and the port reuses it: an earlier revision of this
          ;; rule exited 70 on ANY exhaustion, which made every run of a program
          ;; with no :done? a false alarm. The discriminator is DECLARATION.
          ((PbRd)
            (if (rc-is? r 0)
                (go b PbRd0
                    (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "rdexe") [] (get-s b "work")
                                   (pb-any-of (get-s b "work")) (pb-any-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   300 "/dev/null"))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      (string-concat "examples/replay-demo declares no :done?, so EOF is normal termination, but it did not exit 0; got "
                                     (bs-text r)))))))
          ((PbRd0)
            (if (rc-is? r 0)
                (go b Drv8
                    (seq-commands
                      (wasi.io.stdout (pass-line "argv on RList; :done? exits 42; starved exits 70; no-:done? exits 0"))
                      (drv8-build b)))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      (string-concat "examples/replay-demo did not exit 0 on immediate EOF; got "
                                     (bs-text r)))))))
          ;; stage 8. The cover is a Python program and its exit status is the
          ;; whole verdict; see the helper block's note on why the cells are
          ;; not re-implemented here.
          ((Drv8)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b Drv8S
                    (wasi.proc.run "stack" ["path" "--local-install-root"]
                                   (d-out-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   (d-sir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   (d-sir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   120 "/dev/null"))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "the DRIVER-LL sequencer does not build.")))))
          ((Drv8S)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b Drv8R (wasi.fs.read (d-sir-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      "built the DRIVER-LL sequencer but stack reported no install root for it. Without running it this stage observes nothing, which is the failure mode it exists to prevent.")))))
          ((Drv8R)
            (let [(sir (chomp (bs-text r)))]
              (if (string-empty? sir)
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                        "built the DRIVER-LL sequencer but found no sequencer binary under its install root. Without running it this stage observes nothing, which is the failure mode it exists to prevent.")))
                  (go b Drv8P
                      (wasi.proc.run "python3"
                                     [(d-cover-py (get-s b "root"))
                                      "--driver" (string-concat sir "/bin/sequencer")]
                                     (get-s b "root")
                                     (d-cov-of (get-s b "work"))
                                     (d-cov-of (get-s b "work"))
                                     900 "/dev/null")))))
          ((Drv8P)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b Wav9
                    (seq-commands
                      (wasi.io.stdout (pass-line "DRIVER-LL 4a+4b+4c cover (11 transition cells + 3 manifest shapes + 16 delegated-output cells + 8 content-shape cells + registry)"))
                      (wav9-build b)))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      (string-concat-many
                        ["the DRIVER-LL 4a+4b+4c acceptance cover did not pass. Every scenario is a DECISION the Python reference makes and this port must make identically; the log names the cell and the assertion: "
                         (d-cov-of (get-s b "work"))]))))))
          ;; stage 9.
          ((Wav9)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b Wav9S
                    (wasi.proc.run "stack" ["path" "--local-install-root"]
                                   (w-out-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   (w-sir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   (w-sir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   120 "/dev/null"))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "the DRIVER-LL wave does not build.")))))
          ((Wav9S)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b Wav9R (wasi.fs.read (w-sir-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      "built the DRIVER-LL wave but stack reported no install root for it. Without running it this stage observes nothing, which is the failure mode it exists to prevent.")))))
          ((Wav9R)
            (let [(sir (chomp (bs-text r)))]
              (if (string-empty? sir)
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                        "built the DRIVER-LL wave but found no wave binary under its install root. Without running it this stage observes nothing, which is the failure mode it exists to prevent.")))
                  ;; The cover drives the REAL compiler, not a stub, so the
                  ;; subject passes it the same binary this port resolved for
                  ;; itself at stage 2.
                  (go b Wav9P
                      (wasi.proc.run "python3"
                                     [(w-cover-py (get-s b "root"))
                                      "--wave" (string-concat sir "/bin/wave")
                                      "--llmll" (get-s b "subject")]
                                     (get-s b "root")
                                     (w-cov-of (get-s b "work"))
                                     (w-cov-of (get-s b "work"))
                                     900 "/dev/null")))))
          ((Wav9P)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b Vg10
                    (seq-commands
                      (wasi.io.stdout (pass-line "DRIVER-LL 4e wave cover (7 cells: accept, finding, unfaithful fill, two-brief contention, two usage stops, unsealed tree)"))
                      (vg10-build b)))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      (string-concat-many
                        ["the DRIVER-LL 4e wave cover did not pass. Every cell is a DECISION driver-spec.txt sections 9 and 10 require of the fill protocol; the log names the cell and the assertion: "
                         (w-cov-of (get-s b "work"))]))))))
          ;; stage 10. Two invocations, and the second is the point: a cover of
          ;; fourteen cells proves the gate DISCRIMINATES, and the live run
          ;; proves it accepts the tree it actually ships with.
          ((Vg10)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b Vg10S
                    (wasi.proc.run "stack" ["path" "--local-install-root"]
                                   (v-out-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   (v-sir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   (v-sir-of (get-s b "work"))
                                   120 "/dev/null"))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line "the LLMLL version gate does not build.")))))
          ((Vg10S)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b Vg10R (wasi.fs.read (v-sir-of (get-s b "work"))))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      "built the LLMLL version gate but stack reported no install root for it. Without running it this stage observes nothing, which is the failure mode it exists to prevent.")))))
          ((Vg10R)
            (let [(sir (chomp (bs-text r)))]
              (if (string-empty? sir)
                  (go b Ending
                      (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                        "built the LLMLL version gate but found no versiongate binary under its install root. Without running it this stage observes nothing, which is the failure mode it exists to prevent.")))
                  (go (with-s b "vgexe" (string-concat sir "/bin/versiongate"))
                      Vg10C
                      (wasi.proc.run "python3"
                                     [(v-cover-py (get-s b "root"))
                                      "--gate" (string-concat sir "/bin/versiongate")]
                                     (get-s b "root")
                                     (v-cov-of (get-s b "work"))
                                     (v-cov-of (get-s b "work"))
                                     900 "/dev/null")))))
          ((Vg10C)
            (if (ok? r)
                (go b Vg10F
                    (wasi.fs.write (v-feed-of (get-s b "work")) (v-feed-text)))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      (string-concat-many
                        ["the LLMLL version gate does not agree with scripts/version_gate.sh. The shell script is the reference: a divergence is the port's defect until argued otherwise, and the log names the cell and the two messages: "
                         (v-cov-of (get-s b "work"))]))))))
          ;; The live run's cwd is the WORK directory and not the repo root.
          ;; The gate is a console program and drops its event log beside
          ;; wherever it stands, so running it from the tree would litter a
          ;; tracked directory on every gate run. The subject says the same.
          ((Vg10F)
            (go b Vg10L
                (wasi.proc.run (get-s b "vgexe")
                               ["--root" (get-s b "root")]
                               (get-s b "work")
                               (v-live-of (get-s b "work"))
                               (v-live-of (get-s b "work"))
                               300
                               (v-feed-of (get-s b "work")))))
          ((Vg10L)
            (if (ok? r)
                ;; The ONLY write of the pass mark in this file. See bs-status.
                (go (with-s b "verdict" "pass") Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (pass-line "DRIFT-CI-1 decided by an LLMLL program (14 cover cells + the live tree)")))
                (go b Ending
                    (wasi.io.stdout (fail-line
                      (string-concat-many
                        ["the LLMLL version gate rejected this tree. Its own output: "
                         (v-live-of (get-s b "work"))]))))))
          ((Ending) (go b Done   (wasi.io.stdout "")))
          ((Done)   (go b Done   (wasi.io.stdout "")))))))

  ;; RC-4: the step that trips :done? has its Command discarded, so Done sits
  ;; one past the last emitting phase rather than on it.
  (def-shell bs-done? [s: (Bs, Ctl)] -> bool
    (match s ((pair b p) (match p ((Done) true) ((Boot) false) ((Tool1) false)
                                 ((Tool1Ghc) false) ((Comp2) false) ((Home2) false) ((Comp2b) false)
                                 ((Anchor2a) false) ((Anchor2aR) false) ((Anchor2aC) false) ((Anchor2aCR) false)
                                 ((Anchor2aX1) false) ((Anchor2aX2) false) ((Build3) false) ((Lib4) false) ((Log4) false) ((Build4b) false) ((Lib4b) false) ((Build5) false) ((Sir5) false) ((Sir5R) false) ((Bin5) false) ((Feed5) false)
                                 ((Del5in) false) ((Del5out) false) ((Del5err) false) ((Rmd5) false)
                                 ((Pre5) false) ((Run5) false) ((Out5) false) ((Post5) false)
                                 ((Fe) false) ((FeS) false) ((FeR) false)
                                 ((FeD1) false) ((FeD2) false) ((FeD3) false) ((FeD4) false) ((FeD5) false)
                                 ((FeRm) false) ((FeMk) false) ((FeBin) false) ((FeBig) false)
                                 ((FePlat) false) ((FeUn) false) ((FeRun) false) ((FeOut) false)
                                 ((FeH1) false) ((FeH2) false) ((FeH3) false) ((FeH4) false)
                                 ((Cenc) false) ((CencS) false) ((CencR) false) ((CencRun) false) ((CencOd) false) ((CencP) false)
                                 ((Js) false) ((JsS) false) ((JsR) false) ((JsRun) false) ((JsP) false)
                                 ((Pm) false) ((PmS) false) ((PmR) false) ((PmRun) false) ((PmP) false)
                                 ((PmDone) false) ((PmOut) false) ((PmFin) false)
                                 ((Ps) false) ((PsS) false) ((PsR) false) ((PsRun) false) ((PsOut) false) ((PsFin) false)
                                 ((Rp) false) ((RpCp) false) ((RpB) false) ((RpS) false) ((RpR) false) ((RpF) false)
                                 ((RpRun) false) ((RpLog) false) ((RpRep) false) ((RpOut) false) ((RpTam) false) ((RpTRun) false)
                                 ((RpWi) false) ((RpWiP) false) ((RpWd) false) ((RpFin) false)
                                 ((Pb) false) ((PbS) false) ((PbR) false) ((PbF2) false) ((PbF1) false)
                                 ((PbA) false) ((PbAr) false) ((PbArP) false) ((PbA0) false) ((PbA0P) false)
                                 ((PbSt) false) ((PbDel) false) ((PbEof) false) ((PbHdr) false)
                                 ((PbRd) false) ((PbRd0) false)
                                 ((Drv8) false) ((Drv8S) false) ((Drv8R) false) ((Drv8P) false)
                                 ((Wav9) false) ((Wav9S) false) ((Wav9R) false) ((Wav9P) false)
                                 ((Vg10) false) ((Vg10S) false) ((Vg10R) false) ((Vg10C) false)
                                 ((Vg10F) false) ((Vg10L) false)
                                 ((Ending) false)))))

  ;; THE EXIT STATUS, AND THE DEFAULT IS FAILURE.
  ;;
  ;; Until this shipped the harness PRINTED `BUILD-GATE-1 FAIL:` and exited 0,
  ;; because no :status was declared. Measured on three negative controls: each
  ;; printed its failure line and each answered 0. A gate that cannot fail a
  ;; job is the dead-gate mode this file's header refuses, and it would have
  ;; reached CI the moment the port replaced scripts/build_smoke.sh. The
  ;; sibling ports pathlint.llmll and versiongate.llmll both declare :status;
  ;; this one was the outlier.
  ;;
  ;; WHY THE MARK IS ON THE PASS AND NOT ON THE FAILURES. Forty paths reach
  ;; Ending and thirty-nine of them carry a fail-line. Marking each failure
  ;; makes the fortieth the one that forgets, and a forgotten mark exits 0 on a
  ;; failure it just printed, which is the exact defect being fixed. So the
  ;; record starts unmarked, this function reads 1, and ONLY the last stage's
  ;; success writes "pass". A stage appended after stage 10 inherits the
  ;; failure default rather than an inherited pass, so the next author cannot
  ;; make this mistake by omission.
  ;;
  ;; THE DECISION MOVED OUT AND THE PROJECTION STAYED. `status-of` in
  ;; adjudicate.llmll takes the answer as a plain bool and its contract pins
  ;; both the 0..255 range and the policy in both directions; this function
  ;; reads the mark out of a composite state, which is the part that cannot
  ;; carry a proof (PROC-BOUNDARY-1 records the shape). Splitting them is the
  ;; only way any of it is proved rather than asserted.
  (def-shell bs-status [s: (Bs, Ctl)] -> int
    (match s ((pair b _) (status-of (= (get-s b "verdict") "pass")))))

  (def-main
    :mode    console
    :init    (bs-init)
    :step    bs-step
    :done?   bs-done?
    :status  bs-status))
